\section{Overview}

\subsection{Interface}
\label{sec:overview-interface}

The fitting function \cde{st.lmm} accepts a fixed-effects formula,
random-effects formulas, a residual covariance formula and model data.
Optional arguments specify known covariance matrices, the estimation
method and the inference settings.

The following example uses the rapeseed data distributed with
the package. Genotypes, environments and their interaction are fixed,
replicate effects are random within environments. The residual
variance is common to all observations. The detailed analysis appears in
Section~\ref{sec:rapeseed-trial}. The returned object
\cde{m.rapeseed} stores the fitted model and its results.

<<I01-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-rapeseed", package = "SelectionTools")

m.rapeseed <- st.lmm(
    fixed = hgt ~ geno * env,
    random = ~env:rep,
    data = rapeseed
)
m.rapeseed
@

The function \cde{st.lsm} calculates Least-squares means. The
following call returns genotype means averaged over the environments
in the rapeseed model (\litref{searle1980}{Searle et al., 1980}).

<<I01-lsm,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(m.rapeseed, "geno")
@

In the tropical maize example genoype and block are random factors,
only the intercept is fixed. From the marker data of the maize lines
a genomic relationship matrix is calculated, which is used to model the
covariance between the random  genotype effects using \cde{ucov}.
The genomic analysis appears in Section~\ref{sec:genomic-blup}.

<<I01-blup,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-tropmaize", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-tropmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.tropmaize.vcf, data.set = "maize.markers")
A <- st.get.A("maize.markers")
maize.model <- st.lmm(
    fixed  = y ~ 1,
    random = ~ gen + block,
    ucov   = list(gen = A),
    data   = tropmaize
)
@

The function \cde{st.predict} returns the fixed intercept plus each
predicted genotype effect, giving BLUPs of the genotypic values (\litref{henderson1975}{Henderson, 1975}).

<<I01-predictions,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.predictions <- st.predict(maize.model, "gen")
maize.predictions
@


The chicken example compares diet-specific growth slopes.
The model describes chicken weight as a function of time and diet.
Each diet has its own time slope, while all diets share a common fixed intercept.
Each chick has a random slope deviation from its diet's slope, without a random intercept.
Residual variances may differ among measurement times. The detailed
chicken analyses appear in Section~\ref{sec:chicken-growth}.

<<I01-chicken,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-chickens", package = "SelectionTools")
chickens$weight.s <- sqrt(chickens$weight)
chicken.model <- st.lmm(
    fixed  = weight.s ~ Time:Diet,
    random = ~ (0 + Time) | Chick,
    cov    = ~ at(Time):units,
    data   = chickens
)
@

The slopes for the diets are estimated with \cde{st.lsm}.
<<I01-slopes,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    trend = "Time"
)
@


The one-sided tests assess whether each slope improvement exceeds 0.05
square-root-weight units per day. The argument \cde{rhs} specifies
this nonzero right-hand side, and \cde{side=">"} specifies the
alternative.  Single-step adjustment accounts for correlations among
the contrasts and controls the family-wise error rate (\litref{hothorn2008}{Hothorn et al., 2008}).

<<I01-minimum,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    trend = "Time",
    method = "trt.vs.ctrl",
    ref = "d1",
    rhs = .05,
    side = ">",
    adjust = "single-step"
)
@

The ajdusted mean weight for each diet at Time = 21 is

<<I01-means,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    at = list(Time = 21)
)
@


\subsection{Specifying the model}
\label{sec:overview-model}
\paragraph{Fixed effects.}

The fixed argument is a two-sided formula with a numeric response on the
left: a single response for a univariate model, or several responses combined
with \cde{cbind()} for a multivariate model. A numeric predictor represents a
regression.

These examples illustrate specifying fixed effects, using generic variable names:
\begin{Rcode}
y ~ treatment                 # Treatment main effects and an intercept
y ~ treatment + site          # Additive treatment and site effects
y ~ treatment * site          # Main effects plus their interaction
y ~ treatment:site            # An interaction term
y ~ site/block                # Site plus blocks nested within site
y ~ x + I(x^2)                # Linear and quadratic regression
y ~ 0 + treatment             # Treatment columns without an intercept
y ~ .                         # Automatic fixed-predictor selection from data
\end{Rcode}

The formula operators combine predictors and define their interactions. \cde{a*b} expands to
\cde{a + b + a:b}; \cde{a/b} expands to \cde{a + a:b}. The constants
\cde{1} and \cde{0} include and exclude the intercept. The expression
\cde{I(x\^{}2)}
is an arithmetic square. Outside \cde{I()}, \cde{\^{}} specifies the
highest interaction order: \cde{(a + b + c)\^{}2} includes the three main
effects and all two-way interactions, expanding to
\cde{a + b + c + a:b + a:c + b:c} (\litref{wilkinson1973}{Wilkinson and Rogers, 1973}). In a fixed formula, \cde{.} expands to the other named
predictor columns of the supplied data. For example,
\cde{mpg \textasciitilde{} .} includes the other columns of \cde{mtcars}.
In univariate models, additive
\cde{offset(...)} terms and the \cde{offset} argument supply known mean
contributions.

Factor coding determines how coefficients describe differences between factor
levels. With the usual treatment coding, the first level is the reference.
Sum coding describes deviations from an average of the level means. The
choice changes coefficient interpretation; alternative codings of the same
model give the same fitted values (\litref{searle1987}{Searle, 1987}). The following argument selects sum
coding for the two rapeseed factors:
\begin{Rcode}
contrasts = list(geno = "contr.sum", env = "contr.sum")
\end{Rcode}
The list can name a contrast function or supply a coding matrix for each
factor. With the usual R settings, unordered factors use treatment coding
and ordered factors use polynomial coding. An explicit \cde{contrasts}
entry takes precedence over the factor's contrast attribute, followed by
\cde{options("contrasts")}. The order of the factor levels identifies the
reference level for treatment coding.

Linear and quadratic regression are illustrated with fertilizer doses in
Section~\ref{sec:fertilizer-regression}.
The multiple-regression example with \cde{mtcars} in
Section~\ref{sec:mtcars-subsets} illustrates selection among fixed predictors.


\paragraph{Random effects.}
A one-sided grouping formula adds random intercepts. The levels of each
grouping term are independent unless a matching covariance matrix is supplied
through \cde{ucov}. These examples illustrate specifying random effects:
\begin{Rcode}
~gen + env + gen:env           # Genotype, environment and interaction
~block + block:wholeplot       # Blocks and whole plots within blocks
~region:village               # Villages identified within regions
~env/rep/block                # Environments, replicates and nested blocks
~(0 + Time) | Chick           # Random time slope per chick, without an intercept
~at(materialgroup):gen        # Separate genotype variances by material group
~overlay(female, male)        # Shared parental effects in both parental roles
\end{Rcode}
The term \cde{gen:env} has grouping levels defined by genotype by
environment combinations. A shared variance component is estimated for
those combinations in a univariate model. Nesting is expressed through the
formula and the identifiers. For blocks nested within environments, the
combination of environment and block identifies each experimental block.

The bar in \cde{\textasciitilde{}(0 + Time) | Chick} separates the random
coefficients on its left from the grouping factor on its right. Each chick
has its own random time slope. The \cde{0 +} excludes a random intercept;
\cde{\textasciitilde{}Time | Chick} includes both a random intercept and a
random slope, together with their covariance. The chicken example in
Section~\ref{sec:overview-interface} uses the slope-only specification;
further random-regression examples appear in
Section~\ref{sec:chicken-growth}.

For a univariate response, \cde{at(materialgroup):gen} gives each material
group its own genotype variance. Genotype effects in different groups are
independent. The colon attaches the genotype effects to the groups selected
by \cde{at()}. The material-group example in
Section~\ref{sec:material-groups} uses \cde{grp} as the group variable.

The \cde{overlay(female, male)} operator uses one shared set of parental
effects for both parental roles. Each cross receives the sum of the effects
of its female and male parents, so a parent has the same effect whichever
role it occupies. The half-diallel example in
Section~\ref{sec:half-diallel} illustrates this specification (\litref{sprague1942}{Sprague and Tatum, 1942}).

Nested random factors, as required for incomplete blocks within replicates
in a lattice design, are illustrated with barley in
Section~\ref{sec:barley-lattice-nested}. Its \cde{RowBlk} identifiers already
distinguish blocks within replicates. Correlated random genotype effects
specified through a genomic relationship matrix are illustrated with
tropical maize in Section~\ref{sec:genomic-blup}.

Omitting \cde{random}, or setting it to \cde{NULL}, gives a fixed-only
Gaussian model. A list combines
grouping and random-regression formulas. This example combines block
effects with subject-specific random intercepts and time slopes:
\begin{Rcode}
random = list(~block, ~Time | subject)
\end{Rcode}
The list combines these terms in one fitted model. Fixed genotype effects
give adjusted genotype means; random genotype effects give BLUPs under
the specified genotype covariance. The tropical-maize example in Section~\ref{sec:overview-interface}
illustrates genotype prediction with related random effects.

\paragraph{Repeated measurements and related genotypes.}
There are two distinct kinds of correlation. A random regression can estimate
correlation between coefficients belonging to the same subject
(\litref{laird1982}{Laird and Ware, 1982}). A supplied
relationship matrix can describe correlation between different subjects or
genotypes. They can also be combined.

The formula \cde{random = \textasciitilde{}Time | Chick} gives every chick an
intercept and
a slope, estimating their two variances and their covariance. The formula
\cde{\textasciitilde{}0 + Time | Chick} keeps a random slope only. The formula
\cde{\textasciitilde{}diag(1 + Time) | Chick} retains both coefficients but
fixes their
covariance to zero. Independent chicks can therefore have correlated
intercept and slope coefficients within each chick. Centering a time
covariate changes the interpretation of the intercept and its covariance
with the slope. The chicken example in
Section~\ref{sec:overview-interface} uses a random slope for each chick.

For related genotypes, \cde{ucov = list(gen = A)} supplies their
relationship matrix. The matrix names identify the random grouping terms.
The tropical-maize example in Section~\ref{sec:overview-interface}
illustrates this use.

\paragraph{Residuals.}
Residuals are the variation left after the fixed and random terms. The
default \cde{cov = \textasciitilde{}units} gives one residual variance for a
single response. The word \cde{units} is reserved in covariance formulas:
it identifies the original data rows and does not require a column named
\cde{units} in the data. Different rows have
independent residuals in these \cde{units} structures. Shared random
effects can still correlate their observations.

The formula \cde{cov = \textasciitilde{}at(env):units} estimates a separate
residual variance for each environment. Each such term contains
\cde{units}. Random blocks describe shared block deviations; heterogeneous
residual variances describe differences in the remaining variability.

Measurements from the same individual or nearby plots may remain correlated
after fitting the random effects. Residual covariance formulas can describe
that pattern; the repeated-measurement and spatial examples illustrate the
available structures.
\paragraph{Multivariate responses.}
A matrix response, for example \cde{cbind(GYLD, TKW, PLH)}, fits several
traits jointly. Each trait has its own fixed-effect coefficients, while
the random and residual blocks describe covariance across traits. The
symbol \cde{trait} is reserved in covariance formulas and indexes the
response columns in their \cde{cbind()} order. The response columns
themselves supply this trait index. The joint maize analysis in
Section~\ref{sec:multivariate-maize} illustrates genetic and residual
covariances between traits.

\subsection{Specifying variance structures}
\label{sec:variance-structures}
Variance structures describe how variability differs between groups and
which measurements or outcomes may be correlated. The fixed-effects formula
still describes their expected values. A common residual variance is the
default; separate variances can be fitted for locations or other groups.
For several traits, \cde{us(trait)} allows correlations between traits,
whereas \cde{diag(trait)} sets those correlations to zero in that component (\litref{searle1992}{Searle et al., 1992}).

For one outcome recorded once per subject and visit, a residual formula such
as \cde{\textasciitilde{}ar1(0 + Visit) | Subject} models correlation across
visits within a subject. The factor \cde{Visit} defines their order.
Random slopes describe differences in individual trajectories, as in the
chicken example in Section~\ref{sec:overview-interface}.

Table~\ref{tab:variance-structures} summarizes variance structures. Here, $q$ is the number
of random coefficients or ordered measurement occasions.
AR(1) correlation decreases with the distance between ordered occasions
when its correlation parameter lies between zero and one.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Univariate variance structures and numbers of covariance parameters.}
\label{tab:variance-structures}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{7.3cm}p{6.0cm}r@{}}
\toprule
Modifier or residual formula & Restriction & Parameters \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{Univariate random coefficients or residual occasions: $q$ positions}} \\
\cde{us(...)} & Separate variances and unrestricted covariances & $q(q+1)/2$\\
\cde{diag(...)} & Separate variances, zero covariances & $q$\\
\cde{homdiag(...)} & Common variance, zero covariances & $1$\\
\cde{cs(..., hom=TRUE)} & Common variance and common correlation & $2$\\
\cde{cs(..., hom=FALSE)} & Separate variances and common correlation & $q+1$\\
\cde{ar1(..., hom=TRUE)} & Common variance and AR(1) correlation & $2$\\
\cde{ar1(..., hom=FALSE)} & Separate variances and AR(1) correlation & $q+1$\\
\addlinespace[0.8em]
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{Spatial residual covariance}} \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}ar1(Row):ar1(Column)} & Common variance, with separate AR(1) correlations along rows and columns & $3$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Separable AR(1) by AR(1) residuals are illustrated with barley in
Section~\ref{sec:barley-ar1xar1}. The chicken analysis in
Section~\ref{sec:chicken-time-variance} combines random regression with
separate residual variances for measurement times.

\paragraph{Different experimental designs.}
For a univariate response, experiment A can have spatial AR(1) by AR(1)
residuals, while experiment B has random replicates and blocks nested
within replicates, together with independent residuals. The factor
\cde{experiment} identifies the experiments; \cde{Row} and \cde{Column}
identify plot coordinates, and \cde{rep} and \cde{block} identify the
replicates and incomplete blocks. The model arguments are:
\begin{Rcode}
random = ~(rep/block):at(experiment, "B"),
cov = list(
    ~(ar1(Row):ar1(Column)) | at(experiment, "A"),
    ~units:at(experiment, "B")
)
\end{Rcode}
The selectors restrict each structure to its experiment.
Spatial residuals are illustrated in Section~\ref{sec:barley-ar1xar1},
and nested incomplete blocks in Section~\ref{sec:barley-lattice-nested}.

\subsection{Multivariate models}
\label{sec:overview-multivariate}
A multivariate model analyses several recorded outcomes together, such as
yield and dry-matter content. \cde{cbind()} identifies those response columns.
The reserved covariance symbol \cde{trait} refers directly to those response columns. A joint analysis can estimate relationships between
outcomes while reporting results for each outcome (\litref{anderson2003}{Anderson, 2003}).

Table~\ref{tab:multivariate-variance-structures} summarizes residual
covariance structures for multivariate responses. Here, $T$ is the number
of traits, $L$ the number of locations and $E$ the number of environments.
Bare \cde{units} uses an unstructured trait covariance matrix.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Multivariate residual variance structures and numbers of covariance parameters.}
\label{tab:multivariate-variance-structures}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{7.3cm}p{6.0cm}r@{}}
\toprule
Residual formula & Restriction & Parameters \\
\midrule
\cde{\textasciitilde{}units} & One common unstructured trait matrix. & $T(T+1)/2$ \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}diag(trait):units} & Separate trait variances, without trait covariance. & $T$ \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}at(loc):units} & A full trait covariance matrix for each location. & $LT(T+1)/2$ \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}diag(trait):at(loc):units} & A separate variance for every trait and location. & $LT$ \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}at(env):units} & A full trait matrix for every defined environment. & $ET(T+1)/2$ \\
\cde{\textasciitilde{}diag(trait):at(env):units} & A separate variance for every trait and environment. & $ET$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Unstructured covariance between traits is illustrated in the maize analysis
in Section~\ref{sec:multivariate-maize}. The three-trait barley analysis in
Section~\ref{sec:barley-trait-strata} estimates a separate residual trait
covariance matrix for each environment using \cde{at(env):units}.

The following example uses the barley multi-environment data. A separate
unstructured residual covariance matrix for each location allows residual
correlations between traits. The detailed three-trait analysis appears in
Section~\ref{sec:barley-trait-strata}:
\begin{Rcode}
data("v-lmm-barley-met", package = "SelectionTools")
m.multi <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(GYLD, TKW, PLH) ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + loc + year + gen:loc + gen:year + loc:year +
        gen:loc:year + year:loc:rep,
    cov = ~us(trait):at(loc):units,
    data = barley.met,
    control = list(maxit = 1000)
)
\end{Rcode}
The setting \cde{control = list(maxit = 1000)} allows the optimizer up to
1,000 iterations when estimating variance and covariance parameters.
Fitting stops earlier when convergence is reached. A larger iteration
limit permits more iterations but does not guarantee convergence.


\paragraph{Separate variances for groups.}
For a single response, \cde{\textasciitilde{}units} has one residual variance
and
\cde{\textasciitilde{}at(loc):units} has one per location. Similarly,
\cde{random = \textasciitilde{}at(grp):gen} gives independent genotype
components in the
groups defined by \cde{grp}. The fitted fixed mean still comes from
\cde{fixed}.

The operator \cde{at(factor)} creates a separate covariance block for each
observed level of the specified factor. For a location factor named
\cde{loc}, \cde{at(loc, "HOH")} selects only location HOH, while
\cde{at(loc, c("HOH", "EWE"))} creates separate blocks for those two
locations. A selected location contributes residual variation only for
observations at that location.

The following specification assigns separate trait variances to HOH and
EWE and unstructured trait covariance matrices to EIN, OLI and HFH.
\begin{Rcode}
cov = list(
    ~diag(trait):at(loc, c("HOH", "EWE")):units,
    ~us(trait):at(loc, c("EIN", "OLI", "HFH")):units
)
\end{Rcode}

\subsection{Model selection}
\label{sec:overview-selection}
Model selection compares alternative sets of fixed predictors. The
\cde{selection} list specifies the search method, the largest number of
predictors considered through \cde{nvmax}, and the comparison criterion.
An exhaustive search examines all eligible subsets up to that size;
forward and backward searches examine a sequence of models (\litref{draper1998}{Draper and Smith, 1998}).

\cde{summary(model.selection)} displays the comparison. The specified full
model remains fitted. The chosen subset can be fitted with its own formula
in a subsequent \cde{st.lmm} call, as illustrated in
Section~\ref{sec:mtcars-subsets}.

The following example compares subsets of five predictors of fuel economy.
The smallest \cde{BICm} identifies the preferred retained model. The information-criterion approach is described by
\litref{akaike1974}{Akaike (1974)} and \litref{schwarz1978}{Schwarz (1978)}.
<<I02-selection,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-mtcars", package = "SelectionTools")
model.selection <- st.lmm(
    mpg ~ disp + hp + wt + qsec + am,
    data = mtcars,
    method = "moments",
    selection = list(
        search = "exhaustive",
        nvmax = 5,
        criterion = "BICm"
    )
)
summary(model.selection)
@

\subsection{Tests of model parameters}
\label{sec:overview-tests}
\subsubsection{Fixed parameters}
Fixed-effect tests assess whether a model term contributes to the expected
response after accounting for the other terms. A factor term usually involves
several coefficients, which are tested jointly; a regression term can test a
slope. The default Type III tests assess each term conditional on the other
model terms. In a model containing interactions, the tested hypothesis also
depends on those interactions and the factor coding.
The call \cde{anova(m)} returns the fitted model's fixed-term tests.
Explicit coefficient hypotheses are available through \cde{st.lsm} with
\cde{L} and \cde{joint=TRUE} (\litref{searle1987}{Searle, 1987}).

For complete balanced categorical designs with independent random intercepts
and homogeneous errors, fixed-effect ANOVA terms use exact F-tests where an
expected-mean-square denominator is available. The output labels these tests
as exact, including when variance components are estimated by REML. Different
factors can use different denominators, as in a split-plot experiment.
For other terms, an F or Wald statistic compares the estimated effect
with its uncertainty under the null hypothesis. The default
Satterthwaite method then approximates denominator degrees of freedom while
accounting for estimated covariance parameters
(\litref{satterthwaite1946}{Satterthwaite, 1946}). For suitable REML models,
\cde{ddf="Kenward-Roger"} also adjusts the covariance of the fixed-effect
estimates and scales the F statistic
(\litref{kenward1997}{Kenward and Roger, 1997}). The setting
\cde{ddf="asymptotic"} uses a large-sample Wald reference, displayed with
infinite denominator degrees of freedom. These methods assess the same
specified effects with different approximations to their uncertainty.

For balanced moment analyses, fixed-effect F tests use the expected mean
squares to identify an appropriate error term. That denominator can differ
between experimental factors, as in a split-plot experiment. For fixed-only
moment models, \cde{anova(m1, m2)} compares nested models by an F test
(\litref{cornfield1956}{Cornfield and Tukey, 1956};
\litref{underwood1997}{Underwood, 1997}).

\subsubsection{Random parameters}
A random-term test asks whether the variation represented by an entire
random term is needed. For example, testing a random genotype term asks
whether its variance is zero. For a random regression or a multivariate
term, all associated coefficients and covariance parameters are assessed
together. The call \cde{anova(m, effects="random")} returns these tests;
\cde{effects="both"} combines fixed, random and any joint tests in one table.

For likelihood fits, the default \cde{random.test="auto"} uses exact F-tests
when a complete balanced categorical design provides an appropriate
expected-mean-square denominator for every random term. The REML or ML
variance estimates are retained. The explicit setting \cde{random.test="F"}
selects this exact analysis. In the regions and villages example, the
village-within-region mean square is compared with the residual mean square;
the fixed region effect instead uses the village-within-region mean square.

For other designs, \cde{random.test="auto"} uses likelihood-ratio tests.
The explicit setting \cde{random.test="LRT"} selects these tests. Each compares
the full model with a reduced model omitting the tested term. The statistic is twice
the difference in log likelihood, with the same observations, fixed effects
and estimation method in both fits. REML models therefore use REML
likelihoods for this comparison. The reported chi-squared approximation uses
the difference in the number of free covariance parameters as its degrees
of freedom. Zero variance lies on the boundary of the parameter space, so
this reference distribution is approximate
(\litref{self1987}{Self and Liang, 1987}).

The option \cde{random.test="bootstrap"} obtains a simulation-based
reference distribution. Responses are simulated under the reduced model,
and both models are fitted to each simulated response. The p-value measures
how often the simulated likelihood-ratio statistic reaches or exceeds the
observed statistic. The simulation count is set by \cde{control\$bootstrap};
\cde{set.seed()} provides a reproducible starting state. The returned results
report Monte Carlo uncertainty and failed fits. This approach requires many
more fits (\litref{davison1997}{Davison and Hinkley, 1997}).
For balanced moment analyses, random-term tests instead use expected mean
squares and the corresponding F-test denominators. The setting
\cde{random.test="none"} omits random-term testing.

\subsection{Least-squares means and reference grids}
\label{sec:overview-means}

Least-squares means (in the terminology of SAS and the older R package
\cde{lsmeans}) or expected marginal means (in the terminology of the
newer R packge \cde{emmeans}) describe the expected response of a
treatment under conditions specified by the fixed effects of the model
(\litref{lenth2016}{Lenth, 2016}). Pairwise comparisons, treatment vs.\ control
comparisons, or user defined contrast matrices test hypotheses on
these least-squares means.
The reference grid contains the factor combinations and numeric predictor
values used to calculate  the least-squares means. 
Predictor names or a one-sided formula select the reported means.

In the rapeseed example we investigated genotypes at two fixed
enviroments (drought stress and no stress). The trial and its genotype
means are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:rapeseed-trial}.
\begin{Rcode}
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-rapeseed", package = "SelectionTools")
m.rapeseed <- st.lmm(
    fixed = hgt ~ geno * env,
    random = ~env:rep,
    data = rapeseed
)
\end{Rcode}

\cde{\textasciitilde{}geno} gives a reference grid of genotype means
averaged equally over environments. The formula
\cde{\textasciitilde{}geno * env} gives a mean for every
genotype--environment combination in a combined grid.
The formula \cde{\textasciitilde{}geno | env} gives the same means,
grouped by environment.


\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(m.rapeseed, ~geno)
st.lsm(m.rapeseed, ~geno * env)
st.lsm(m.rapeseed, ~geno | env)
\end{Rcode}



The grouping determines how subsequent comparisons are organized.
Pairwise comparisons based on \cde{\textasciitilde{}geno * env}
include pairs of genotype--environment combinations across the
combined grid, with a respective multiple testing adjustment.
Pairwise comparisons based on \cde{\textasciitilde{}geno | env}
compare genotypes within each environment, with multiplicity
adjustment applied separately within each environment by default.


The chicken eperiment compares weight gain of four chicken diets. The
diets are fixed regression slopes, and each chicken gets a random
slope for its individual growth, the residuals have different
variances at the times when chicken weight was measured.  We
investigate the square root of thicken weight to make the estimated
regression lines look more linear. The growth models are discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:chicken-growth}.

\begin{Rcode}
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-chickens", package = "SelectionTools")
chickens$weight.s <- sqrt(chickens$weight)
chicken.model <- st.lmm(
    fixed = weight.s ~ Time:Diet,
    random = ~ (0 + Time) | Chick,
    cov = ~at(Time):units,
    data = chickens
)
\end{Rcode}


In the chicken trial, \cde{at=list(Time=21)} estimates the mean response
for each diet at day 21. The reference grid fixes \cde{Time} at 21 and
includes each diet. 


\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    at = list(Time = 21)
)
\end{Rcode}

The option \cde{trend="Time"} instead estimates the
growth slope for each diet.

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    trend = "Time"
)
\end{Rcode}

The chicken model uses square-root weight as its
response. Back-transformation expresses the least-squares means and their
confidence intervals in the original weight units. The estimated
square-root means and interval endpoints are truncated at zero and
then squared, while standard
errors are converted using the delta method. Tests remain on the
square-root scale. These back-transformed least-squares means describe the fitted
growth curves on the weight scale. Estimating arithmetic mean weight
additionally requires a correction for variation among chicks and
residual variation. The square-root growth analysis appears in
Section~\ref{sec:chicken-growth}.

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    chicken.model,
    "Diet",
    at = list(Time = 21),
    transform = "sqrt",
    type = "response"
)
\end{Rcode}





The standard error of a least-squares mean depends on the inference
space, which specifies the population the mean describes. The default
\cde{inference.space = "broad"} averages over the random effects.
With \cde{inference.space = "narrow"}, the mean describes the particular
groups in the experiment for all random terms. A formula such as
\cde{inference.space = ~operator} gives an intermediate mean for the
operators in the experiment while averaging over the other random
effects. Section~\ref{sec:machine-inference} compares the standard
errors for these choices using the same model fit.

\subsection{Test of hypotheses on least-squares means}
\label{sec:overview-pairwise}

\paragraph{Pairwise and control comparisons.}

The fertilizer experiment  estimates mean yield for each treatment.
The detailed comparisons appear in Section~\ref{sec:fertilizer-contrasts}.

\begin{Rcode}
data("v-lmm-fertilizer", package = "SelectionTools")
m.fert <- st.lmm(
    yield ~ fert,
    data = fertilizer
)
\end{Rcode}

The pairwise comparison tests yield differences between all treatment
pairs. Hochberg adjustment controls the family-wise error rate under its
dependence assumptions (\litref{hochberg1988}{Hochberg, 1988}).

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    m.fert,
    "fert",
    method = "pairwise",
    adjust = "hochberg"
)
\end{Rcode}

The one-sided Dunnett comparisons test whether each treatment yields
more than the control.
The argument \cde{ref=1} selects the first treatment in the means grid.
The multivariate \(t\) adjustment accounts for the correlations between
contrasts that share the control
(\litref{dunnett1955}{Dunnett, 1955} and
\litref{genz2009}{Genz and Bretz, 2009}).

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    m.fert,
    "fert",
    method = "trt.vs.ctrl",
    ref = 1,
    side = ">",
    adjust = "mvt"
)
\end{Rcode}

\paragraph{User-defined comparisons.}

In the oats split-plot experiment, varieties occupy whole plots within
blocks and nitrogen treatments occupy subplots. The detailed oats analysis
appears in Section~\ref{sec:oats-splitplot}.

\begin{Rcode}
data("v-lmm-oats", package = "SelectionTools")
m.oats.contrasts <- st.lmm(
    fixed = Yield ~ Variety * Nitro,
    random = ~Block + Block:Variety,
    data = oats
)
\end{Rcode}

The Least-squares means
for nitrogen treatments average equally over varieties.

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(m.oats.contrasts, ~Nitro)
\end{Rcode}

 The contrast
compares the average yield of the three fertilized treatments with the
unfertilized treatment. Its coefficients follow the nitrogen-level order
\cde{n0.0}, \cde{n0.2}, \cde{n0.4}, \cde{n0.6}.

\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    m.oats.contrasts,
    ~Nitro,
    method = list(fertilized_vs_unfertilized = c(-1, 1/3, 1/3, 1/3))
)
\end{Rcode}

\paragraph{Contrasts with fixed right-hand sides.}
The joint F test assesses two nitrogen-treatment differences simultaneously.
The null hypothesis specifies that the yield difference between
\cde{n0.2} and \cde{n0.0} equals 10 and that the difference between
\cde{n0.6} and \cde{n0.0} equals 30, in yield units.
The two values in \cde{rhs} follow the order of the contrasts.
The underlying split-plot analysis appears in
Section~\ref{sec:oats-splitplot}.
\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm(
    m.oats.contrasts,
    ~Nitro,
    method = list(
        low_vs_zero = c(-1, 1, 0, 0),
        high_vs_zero = c(-1, 0, 0, 1)
    ),
    rhs = c(10, 30),
    joint = TRUE
)
\end{Rcode}

\paragraph{Letter displays.}
A compact letter display summarizes the Tukey-adjusted pairwise comparisons.
Treatments that share a letter have no significant difference at the 5\%
level. Treatments with no letter in common differ significantly
(\litref{piepho2004}{Piepho, 2004}). The fertilizer example in
Section~\ref{sec:fertilizer-contrasts} includes letter displays.
\begin{Rcode}
st.lsm.cld(st.lsm(m.fert, "fert"))
\end{Rcode}

\subsection{Best linear unbiased prediction of random effects}
\label{sec:overview-prediction}
Best linear unbiased predictions (BLUPs) estimate the random effects in a
mixed model. They account for the fitted variance components and the
information available for each effect
(\litref{henderson1975}{Henderson, 1975}).

The half-diallel model contains a shared parental effect for general
combining ability (GCA) and a cross effect for specific combining ability
(SCA). The overlay adds the effects of the two parents of each cross
(\litref{sprague1942}{Sprague and Tatum, 1942}).
The example is discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:half-diallel}.

\begin{Rcode}
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-halfdiallel", package = "SelectionTools")
m.diallel <- st.lmm(
    fixed = sugar ~ 1,
    random = ~overlay(female, male) + cross,
    data = halfdiallel
)
\end{Rcode}
This prediction returns each parent's GCA effect.
\begin{Rcode}
st.predict(
    m.diallel,
    "0 + overlay(female, male)"
)
\end{Rcode}
The second returns each cross's SCA effect. The \cde{0 +} prefix
requests the random effects alone.
\begin{Rcode}
st.predict(
    m.diallel,
    "0 + cross"
)
\end{Rcode}
In the tropical-maize trial, genotype and block effects are random.
The marker-based relationship matrix \cde{A} describes the covariance
between genotype effects and enters the model through \cde{ucov}
(\litref{vanraden2008}{VanRaden, 2008}). The detailed genomic analysis
appears in Section~\ref{sec:genomic-blup}.
\begin{Rcode}
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-tropmaize", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-tropmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.tropmaize.vcf, data.set = "maize.markers")
A <- st.get.A("maize.markers")
maize.model <- st.lmm(
    fixed = y ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + block,
    ucov = list(gen = A),
    data = tropmaize
)
\end{Rcode}
The prediction call returns the fixed intercept plus each predicted
genotype effect, giving genomic BLUPs of the genotypic values.
\begin{Rcode}
st.predict(
    maize.model,
    "gen"
)
\end{Rcode}

Prediction output includes prediction-error standard errors and
pointwise confidence intervals, with fitted variance parameters held fixed.



\subsection{Results, printing and serialization}

\label{sec:overview-results}

Table~\ref{tab:result-methods} summarizes methods and components
that provide access to fitted results.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Methods and components for accessing fitted model results.}
\label{tab:result-methods}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{6.2cm}p{8.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
Command or component & Contents \\
\midrule
\cde{coef(m)}, \cde{vcov(m)} & Fixed coefficients and their covariance. \\
\cde{confint(m)} & Normal-reference intervals for fixed coefficients. \\
\cde{fitted(m)}, \cde{residuals(m)} & Matrices with observations in rows and traits in columns. The option \cde{type="marginal"} uses the fixed-effects mean. \\
\cde{formula(m)}, \cde{model.matrix(m)} & Fixed formula and its design matrix. \\
\cde{anova(m)} & Cached fixed-term tests. \\
\cde{anova(m, effects="both")} & A common table of fixed, random and joint tests. \\
\cde{logLik(m)}, \cde{AIC(m)}, \cde{BIC(m)} & Log-likelihood and tables of fit criteria with a row per model. \\
\cde{nobs(m)}, \cde{df.residual(m)} & Observed response cells and their count minus the fixed-design rank. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "observations")} & Retained rows, response cells, fixed rank, and error-stratum DF when available. \\
\cde{predict(m)} & A list with predictions in \cde{fit}, a matrix with observations in rows and traits in columns. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "variance")} & A matrix with a row per block and a column per trait. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "covariance")} & Covariance matrices grouped into random and residual blocks. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "correlation")} & Correlation matrices with the same block groups. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "random")} & Random coefficients, with grouping levels in rows. \\
\cde{st.lmm.components(m, "metadata")} & Convergence and computation diagnostics in \cde{convergence}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A request for a single covariance or correlation block, such as
\cde{block = "gen"}, returns that matrix directly. A request for several
components returns a named list of those components.

Shared printing settings control automatic console display for fitted
models, least-squares means, predictions, test tables and extracted results.
The session-wide defaults are listed in Table~\ref{tab:printing-options}.
The function \cde{st.options()} returns the current settings as a visible
named list, so a call without an assignment displays them. A call such as
\cde{st.options(list(print.digits = 3))} changes the named setting and returns
the complete updated list invisibly. Setting options therefore produces no
automatic console output, while the returned list remains available for
assignment. An individual value is available as
\cde{st.options()\$print.digits}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Shared printing options and their initial values.}
\label{tab:printing-options}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{7.2cm}p{1.4cm}p{6.0cm}@{}}
\toprule
Option & Default & Display choice \\
\midrule
\cde{print.digits} & \cde{4} & Decimal places for statistics. \\
\cde{print.char.perline} & \cde{75} & Maximum line width. \\
\cde{print.top} & \cde{10} & Leading table rows. \\
\cde{print.bottom} & \cde{5} & Trailing table rows. \\
\cde{print.cov.blocks.top} & \cde{3} & Leading covariance blocks. \\
\cde{print.cov.blocks.bottom} & \cde{1} & Trailing covariance blocks. \\
\cde{print.ems} & \cde{FALSE} & Expected mean squares in moment-model output. \\
\cde{print.test.construction} & \cde{FALSE} & Numerator and denominator details for moment tests. \\
\cde{print.tests.for.bare.lsmeans} & \cde{FALSE} & Tests against zero for ordinary least-squares means. \\
\cde{print.intercept.test} & \cde{FALSE} & Stored intercept tests in ANOVA tables. \\
\cde{print.LSD} & \cde{TRUE} & LSD beneath least-squares means without displayed tests. \\
\cde{print.HSD} & \cde{TRUE} & HSD beneath least-squares means without displayed tests. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

ANOVA tables omit the intercept test by default. Regression coefficient
reports retain the intercept estimate, standard error and test. The setting
\cde{st.options(list(print.intercept.test = TRUE))} includes an intercept
test where it is stored in the fitted model.

Balanced categorical analyses can also report the least significant
difference (LSD) and the Tukey-based honestly significant difference (HSD).
One or two compact lines identify common comparison strata, for example
\cde{Within env} and \cde{Across env}, at an alpha level of one minus the
confidence level. The options \cde{print.LSD} and \cde{print.HSD} control
their display independently. For a least-squares means result \cde{x},
\cde{attr(summary(x), "thresholds")} returns the available thresholds with
their SE and DF. Thresholds refer to the analysis scale,
including when the means are displayed after back-transformation.

The following example changes the formatting and displays the model
coefficients.

<<PRINT-options,eval=FALSE>>=
st.options()
st.options(list(
    print.digits = 3,
    print.char.perline = 60,
    print.top = 5,
    print.bottom = 2
))
coef(m.rapeseed)
@

Setting either \cde{print.top} or \cde{print.bottom} to \cde{Inf}
displays all rows. All covariance blocks appear when either block limit in
Table~\ref{tab:printing-options} is set to \cde{Inf}. The row limits apply
independently. These settings affect display; stored estimates and test
results retain their full values.

The following conversions return ordinary R values for further work:
\begin{Rcode}
b <- as.numeric(coef(m.rapeseed))
V <- as.matrix(vcov(m.rapeseed))
lsmeans <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(m.rapeseed, ~geno))
\end{Rcode}

Models and their results can be saved and restored with the standard R
serialization functions:
\begin{Rcode}
rapeseed.file <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(m.rapeseed, rapeseed.file)
m.saved <- readRDS(rapeseed.file)
coef(m.saved)
\end{Rcode}

\subsection{Estimation procedures}
\label{sec:overview-estimation}
\subsubsection{Estimation methods: REML, ML and moments}
The argument \cde{method} selects how model parameters are estimated.
Restricted maximum likelihood (REML), the default, estimates variance and
covariance parameters while accounting for estimation of the fixed effects (\litref{patterson1971}{Patterson and Thompson, 1971}).
Maximum likelihood, \cde{method="ML"}, estimates the parameters from the
likelihood and provides a common basis for comparing models with different
fixed effects (\litref{harville1977}{Harville, 1977}). The setting \cde{method="moments"} gives ordinary least
squares for fixed-only regression and ANOVA-based estimates from expected
mean squares following the Cornfield--Tukey rules for balanced experimental
designs, including nested, split-plot and replicated multi-environment trials (\litref{cornfield1956}{Cornfield and Tukey, 1956}).

\subsubsection{Optimizers: Automatic selection, AI and L-BFGS-B}
Likelihood estimation finds variance and covariance parameters iteratively.
The default \cde{control = list(optimizer = "auto")} chooses the optimizer.
For REML models with diagonal free covariance blocks and at most 200 free
covariance parameters, automatic selection uses safeguarded average-information
iterations (AI-REML) (\litref{gilmour1995}{Gilmour et al., 1995}). Other models, including ML fits, use L-BFGS-B.
The setting \cde{optimizer="AI"} also offers AI-REML for models with
unstructured or other structured covariance blocks within the parameter
limit. The setting \cde{optimizer="L-BFGS-B"} selects L-BFGS-B directly (\litref{byrd1995}{Byrd et al., 1995}).
Moment estimation calculates estimates from regression and ANOVA quantities.

The printed fit reports convergence. The option \cde{control\$maxit} sets
the main iteration limit; fitting can stop earlier when convergence is reached.
A larger limit permits more iterations but does not guarantee convergence.

\subsubsection{Backend paths}
The \cde{control} list selects the covariance calculation through
\cde{backend}. These choices give alternative calculations of the same
statistical model.

\paragraph{Automatic selection.}
The default \cde{backend="auto"} chooses a calculation from the covariance
structure and matrix sizes. It first checks for a suitable spectral model,
then considers sparse mixed-model equations and covariance blocks.
The dense calculation handles the remaining models.
For example, repeated measurements can lead to separate subject blocks,
while independent residuals can permit sparse equations.
The selection uses estimated computational costs; the fastest choice can
also depend on the data and BLAS installation.

\paragraph{Dense covariance calculation.}
The setting \cde{backend="dense"} assembles the full covariance matrix of
the observed responses. Cholesky factorization supplies linear solves and
log determinants during estimation (\litref{lapack1999}{Anderson et al., 1999}). This gives a general calculation for
complex random and residual covariance models, including correlated traits.
Dense BLAS operations can be efficient for large matrices, while storage
grows with the square of the number of observed response values.
Automatic selection uses this calculation when the structured alternatives
do not provide an eligible, sufficiently economical calculation.

\paragraph{Block covariance calculation.}
The setting \cde{backend="block"} factors separate observation-covariance
blocks, for example repeated measurements within independent subjects.
Smaller factorizations reduce storage and work, and identical blocks can
reuse a factorization. Suitable designs can also include random effects
connecting the blocks through a smaller coefficient system.
Automatic selection considers this route after spectral and sparse checks;
its cost comparison favors partitions with smaller blocks.
An explicit block request selects the block calculation for a suitable model.

\paragraph{Sparse mixed-model equations.}
The setting \cde{backend="sparse"} uses the nonzero entries of the
mixed-model equations for models with independent residuals and suitable
random-effect designs. It is useful when many observations share relatively
few random coefficients, as in replicated trials or random regressions.
Sparse factorization can reduce storage and calculation when few additional
nonzero entries arise during elimination (\litref{davis2006}{Davis, 2006}). Supplied relationship terms can
enter through a smaller covariance correction. Automatic selection checks
model size and the estimated factorization work before choosing this route.

\paragraph{Spectral calculation.}
The setting \cde{backend="spectral"} uses eigendecompositions for suitable
relationship-kernel models with one positive diagonal residual shape.
Repeated variance evaluations reuse the transformed matrices; verified
product structures can use smaller factor eigensystems and tensor operations.
Automatic selection checks this route first. The option
\cde{eigen.solver="dsyevd"} selects LAPACK DSYEVD and
\cde{eigen.solver="dsyevr"} selects DSYEVR within the spectral calculation.
The default \cde{eigen.solver="auto"} prefers DSYEVD when its workspace
fits the LAPACK integer interface, and uses DSYEVR otherwise (\litref{lapack1999}{Anderson et al., 1999}).

The following rapeseed model uses automatic optimizer selection with the dense
covariance calculation. The trial analysis appears in
Section~\ref{sec:rapeseed-trial}:
<<OPT-optimizers,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-rapeseed", package = "SelectionTools")
model.auto <- st.lmm(
    hgt ~ geno * env,
    random = ~env:rep,
    data = rapeseed,
    control = list(optimizer = "auto", backend = "dense")
)
@

\section{Cross classified and hierarchical Designs}

\subsection{Moment estimators}

\subsubsection{Two fixed factors: Climate and variety}
\mbox{\cde{CM01: RLab-AppStat/v14-u31.R}}

<<CM01-1,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-climate", package = "SelectionTools")
m.climate <- st.lmm(
    yield ~ climate * variety,
    data = climate,
    method = "moments"
)
m.climate
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear model fitted by the method of moments

