Confidence level: 0.9500
\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Inference spaces for machines and operators}
\label{sec:machine-inference}

Two machine types were evaluated twice by each of three operators drawn
from a population of operators. The scores come from the example presented
by \litref{sanders1991}{Sanders (1991)}. The model includes fixed machine
effects, random operator effects and a random machine-by-operator
interaction.

<<MCH-inference-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-machines", package = "SelectionTools")
m.machines <- st.lmm(
    fixed = score ~ machine,
    random = ~operator + machine:operator,
    data = machines
)
@

The inference space determines which population each machine mean
describes (\litref{mclean1991}{McLean et al., 1991}). The default
\cde{inference.space = "broad"} gives a mean for each machine type
averaged over the operator population and machine-by-operator
interactions.

<<MCH-inference-broad,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(m.machines, ~machine)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Least-squares means (Satterthwaite)
  machine  lsmeans      SE    DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  1        50.9483  0.2467  2.97   50.1591   51.7376
  2        51.9567  0.2467  2.97   51.1674   52.7459
Confidence level: 0.9500
LSD=0.9638 HSD=0.9638 (alpha=0.05)
\end{Soutput}

The first intermediate space describes the three operators in the
experiment and averages over the machine-by-operator interactions.
The second describes the machine-by-operator combinations in the
experiment and averages over the operator main effects.

<<MCH-inference-intermediate-operator,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    m.machines,
    ~machine,
    inference.space = ~operator
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Least-squares means (Satterthwaite)
Inference space: intermediate
  machine  lsmeans      SE  DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  1        50.9483  0.1584   2   50.2668   51.6299
  2        51.9567  0.1584   2   51.2751   52.6382
Confidence level: 0.9500
LSD=0.9638 HSD=0.9638 (alpha=0.05)
\end{Soutput}

<<MCH-inference-intermediate-interaction,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    m.machines,
    ~machine,
    inference.space = ~machine:operator
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Least-squares means (Satterthwaite)
Inference space: intermediate
  machine  lsmeans      SE    DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  1        50.9483  0.2094  1.53   49.7229   52.1737
  2        51.9567  0.2094  1.53   50.7313   53.1821
Confidence level: 0.9500
LSD=0.3112 HSD=0.3112 (alpha=0.05)
\end{Soutput}

Narrow inference gives each machine's mean score for these three
operators, including how each operator performed with that machine.
In this balanced experiment, the SE depends on the residual variance
and the six observations per machine.

<<MCH-inference-narrow,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lsm(
    m.machines,
    ~machine,
    inference.space = "narrow"
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Least-squares means (Satterthwaite)
Inference space: narrow
  machine  lsmeans      SE  DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  1        50.9483  0.0899   6   50.7283   51.1684
  2        51.9567  0.0899   6   51.7366   52.1767
Confidence level: 0.9500
LSD=0.3112 HSD=0.3112 (alpha=0.05)
\end{Soutput}

All four estimates of the first machine mean are about 50.95 in this
balanced experiment. The corresponding published SEs are 0.2467 for
broad inference, 0.1584 for the intermediate space including operators,
0.2095 for the intermediate space including interactions, and 0.0899
for narrow inference (\litref{sanders1991}{Sanders, 1991}). The fitted
interaction SE rounds to 0.2094 when calculated from the full precision
variance estimates.

