\subsection{Separable autoregressive plot residuals}
\label{sec:barley-ar1xar1}

The field spans ten rows and fifteen columns. The spatial model uses
their field-wide positions, including adjacency across replicate
boundaries. The colon in the covariance formula specifies the product
of the row and column AR(1) correlation matrices.

<<LMM-barley-ar1xar1,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-barley-lattice", package = "SelectionTools")
m.barley.ar1xar1 <- st.lmm(
    fixed = yield ~ Variety,
    cov = ~ar1(Row):ar1(Column),
    data = barley.lattice
)
m.barley.ar1xar1
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      barley.lattice
  Fixed:     yield ~ Variety
  Random:    None
  Residual:  ~ar1(Row):ar1(Column)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Fixed effects (Satterthwaite):
  Term     DF1    DF2        F        p
  Variety   24  77.75  13.0434  <0.0001

Covariance components:
  Residual: ar1(Row):ar1(Column)
    Separable AR1 x AR1
  Parameter      Estimate
  Variance     38757.0944
  rho(Row)         0.4586
  rho(Column)      0.6838

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                150    R-squared (conditional)      0.3112
  Log-likelihood        -815.1898    RMSE (conditional)         200.1672
  AIC                   1686.3795    Variance: fixed          17509.7379
  BIC                   1770.6773    Variance: random             0.0000
  R-squared (marginal)     0.3112    Variance: residual       38757.0944
\end{Soutput}

For plots at positions $(r,c)$ and $(r',c')$, the residual covariance is
\[
\operatorname{Cov}(e_{rc},e_{r'c'})=
 \sigma_e^2\rho_r^{|r-r'|}\rho_c^{|c-c'|}.
\]
Ordering the ten rows within each of the fifteen columns gives
\[
\mathbf{R}=\sigma_e^2
 \bigl\{\mathbf{A}_{15}(\rho_c)\otimes\mathbf{A}_{10}(\rho_r)\bigr\},
\qquad [\mathbf{A}_m(\rho)]_{ij}=\rho^{|i-j|}.
\]
There is one residual variance and one correlation parameter for each
direction. Correlation decreases with separation in both directions;
plots in different rows and different columns can therefore have nonzero
residual covariance.

Both models use the same 150 plots and fixed variety effects. The REML AIC
favors the spatial model (1686.38 versus 1703.31 for the block model in
Section~\ref{sec:barley-row-column-blocks}), as does BIC (1770.68 versus
1790.61).

