\section{Heritability}
\label{sec:heritability}

\subsection{Balanced barley trial across years and locations}
\mbox{\cde{RLab-PopGen/q046-10-s1.R}}

\subsubsection{Heritability from variance components}

<<HER-barley-data,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-barley-met", package = "SelectionTools")
xtabs(~loc + year, data = barley.met)
@

\begin{Soutput}
     year
loc   y2016 y2017 y2018
  HOH    60    60    60
  EWE    60    60    60
  EIN    60    60    60
  OLI    60    60    60
  HFH    60    60    60
\end{Soutput}



<<HER-barley-variance,eval=FALSE>>=
m.barley.reml <- st.lmm(
    fixed = TKW ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + loc + year + gen:loc + gen:year + loc:year +
        gen:loc:year + rep:loc:year,
    data = barley.met
)
v <- st.lmm.components(m.barley.reml, "variance")[, "TKW"]
v.g <- as.numeric(v[["gen"]])
v.g
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 11.72524
\end{Soutput}




<<HER-barley-components,eval=FALSE>>=
L <- nlevels(barley.met$loc)
Y <- nlevels(barley.met$year)
R <- nlevels(barley.met$rep)
v.masking <- v[["gen:loc"]] / L +
    v[["gen:year"]] / Y +
    v[["gen:loc:year"]] / (L * Y) +
    v[["units"]] / (L * Y * R)
v.masking
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 5.634385
\end{Soutput}



<<HER-barley-heritability,eval=FALSE>>=
heritability <- v.g / (v.g + v.masking)
heritability
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.6754316
\end{Soutput}




\subsubsection{Heritability from the SE of genotype differences}

<<HER-barley-means-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
m.barley.means <- st.lmm(
    fixed = TKW ~ gen + loc + year + loc:year + year:loc:rep,
    random = ~gen:loc + gen:year + gen:loc:year,
    data = barley.met
)
@

The pairwise comparisons supply the SE of every genotype difference (SED).
Half their mean square estimates the masking variance in the BLUE formula
discussed by \litref{piepho2007}{Piepho and M\"ohring (2007, Eq.~19)}.
It agrees with the variance-component calculation in this balanced trial.

<<HER-barley-means,eval=FALSE>>=
barley.comparisons <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(
    m.barley.means,
    ~gen,
    method = "pairwise",
    adjust = "none"
))
v.masking <- mean(barley.comparisons$SE^2) / 2
v.masking
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 5.634385
\end{Soutput}



<<HER-barley-means-heritability,eval=FALSE>>=
heritability <- v.g / (v.g + v.masking)
heritability
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.6754316
\end{Soutput}




\subsubsection{Heritability from prediction errors of BLUP differences}

The random-genotype fit also supplies BLUPs and their joint prediction-error
covariance. The following calculation uses the mean prediction-error variance
of their pairwise differences. The Cullis measure
(\litref{cullis2006}{Cullis et al., 2006}), discussed by
\litref{piepho2007}{Piepho and M\"ohring (2007, Eq.~20)},
is one minus this variance divided by twice the genetic variance.

<<HER-barley-blup-errors,eval=FALSE>>=
PEV <- vcov(st.predict(m.barley.reml, "0 + gen"))
PEV.differences <- outer(diag(PEV), diag(PEV), "+") - 2 * PEV
v.diff <- mean(PEV.differences[upper.tri(PEV.differences)])
v.diff
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 7.611283
\end{Soutput}


<<HER-barley-blup-heritability,eval=FALSE>>=
heritability <- 1 - v.diff / (2 * v.g)
heritability
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.6754316
\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Barley with missing location--year combinations}

<<HER-missing-data,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-met-missing", package = "SelectionTools")
xtabs(~loc + year, data = met.missing, subset = !is.na(YLD))
@

\begin{Soutput}
      year
loc    y2017 y2018 y2019
  loc1     0     0    80
  loc2    80    80    80
  loc3    80    80    79
  loc4     0     0    80
  loc5     0     0    79
\end{Soutput}




<<HER-missing-variance,eval=FALSE>>=
met.vc <- st.lmm(
    fixed = YLD ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + loc + year + gen:loc + gen:year + loc:year +
        gen:loc:year + year:loc:rep,
    data = met.missing
)
v <- st.lmm.components(met.vc, "variance")
v.g <- as.numeric(v["gen", "YLD"])
v.g
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 56531.11
\end{Soutput}




The factor \cde{REP} identifies the location--year--replicate blocks present
in the trial. With \cde{rep*year*loc} in the fixed part, the default
reference grid also includes combinations for trials that were never
conducted, making the genotype means non-estimable. With \cde{REP}, the
means are averaged over the observed blocks and their SEs can be
calculated
(\hyperref[sec:met-combined-field]{Section~\ref*{sec:met-combined-field}}).

<<HER-missing-means-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
met.missing$REP <- with(met.missing, rep:year:loc)
met.means <- st.lmm(
    fixed = YLD ~ gen + REP,
    random = ~gen:loc + gen:year + gen:loc:year,
    data = met.missing
)
@

Half the mean squared SE of the genotype differences estimates the masking
variance in this unbalanced trial.

<<HER-missing-means,eval=FALSE>>=
met.comparisons <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(
    met.means,
    ~gen,
    method = "pairwise",
    adjust = "none"
))
v.masking <- mean(met.comparisons$SE^2) / 2
v.masking
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 38385.1
\end{Soutput}



<<HER-missing-heritability,eval=FALSE>>=
heritability <- v.g / (v.g + v.masking)
heritability
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.5955896
\end{Soutput}





The same BLUP prediction-error calculation uses the random-genotype model
\cde{met.vc} and its estimate of genetic variance.

<<HER-missing-blup-errors,eval=FALSE>>=
PEV <- vcov(st.predict(met.vc, "0 + gen"))
PEV.differences <- outer(diag(PEV), diag(PEV), "+") - 2 * PEV
v.diff <- mean(PEV.differences[upper.tri(PEV.differences)])
v.diff
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 44574.21
\end{Soutput}


<<HER-missing-blup-heritability,eval=FALSE>>=
heritability <- 1 - v.diff / (2 * v.g)
heritability
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0.6057551
\end{Soutput}


\section{Multivariate Experiments}

\subsection{Moment estimators}

\subsubsection{Two species and two traits: Hotelling test}
\mbox{\cde{CM09: RLab-BioStat/v19-u36.R}}

\medskip\noindent
The Hotelling--Lawley trace gives the exact \(F\) test for these two
species. The other multivariate statistics give the same \(F\) and \(p\)
for this comparison.

<<CM09-1,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-iris-two", package = "SelectionTools")
m.hotelling <- st.lmm(
    cbind(SepalLength, SepalWidth) ~ Species,
    data = iris.two,
    method = "moments"
)
anova(m.hotelling, effects = "manova", test = "Hotelling-Lawley")
@

\begin{Soutput}
MANOVA for Species
  Method            Statistic  DF1  DF2       F       p  Reference
  Hotelling-Lawley     0.6194    2   17  5.2650  0.0166  exact F  
\end{Soutput}

\subsubsection{Three species and four traits: MANOVA}
\mbox{\cde{CM10: RLab-BioStat/v19-u36.R}}

\medskip\noindent
The MANOVA table reports the four multivariate statistics for species
and the reference calculation for each \(F\) statistic.

<<CM10-1,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-iris", package = "SelectionTools")
m.iris <- st.lmm(
    cbind(SepalLength, SepalWidth, PetalLength, PetalWidth) ~ Species,
    data = iris.manova,
    method = "moments"
)
anova(m.iris, effects = "manova")
@

\begin{Soutput}
MANOVA for Species
  Method            Statistic  DF1  DF2         F        p  Reference    
  Pillai               1.1919    8  290   53.4665  <0.0001  approximate F
  Wilks                0.0234    8  288  199.1453  <0.0001  approximate F
  Hotelling-Lawley    32.4773    8  286  580.5321  <0.0001  approximate F
  Roy                 32.1919    4  145     >1000  <0.0001  F upper bound
\end{Soutput}

The \cde{sscp.H} and \cde{sscp.E} components give the sums-of-squares
and cross-products matrices for the species hypothesis and the error.

<<CM10-2,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lmm.components(m.iris, "sscp.H", term = "Species")
@

\begin{Soutput}
               SepalLength  SepalWidth  PetalLength  PetalWidth
  SepalLength      63.2121    -19.9527     165.2484     71.2793
  SepalWidth      -19.9527     11.3449     -57.2396    -22.9327
  PetalLength     165.2484    -57.2396     437.1028    186.7740
  PetalWidth       71.2793    -22.9327     186.7740     80.4133
\end{Soutput}

<<CM10-2-b,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lmm.components(m.iris, "sscp.E")
@

\begin{Soutput}
               SepalLength  SepalWidth  PetalLength  PetalWidth
  SepalLength      38.9562     13.6300      24.6246      5.6450
  SepalWidth       13.6300     16.9620       8.1208      4.8084
  PetalLength      24.6246      8.1208      27.2226      6.2718
  PetalWidth        5.6450      4.8084       6.2718      6.1566
\end{Soutput}

\subsubsection{Multivariate maize: Genetic covariances and correlations}
\label{sec:multivariate-maize}
\mbox{\cde{M60: RLab-BioStat/v19-u36.R, section 4}}

\medskip\noindent
Each genotype has one observation in every environment and replicate.
The ANOVA strata separate the sources of variation in this balanced
design. The sums of squares and cross-products in each stratum, divided
by its DF, form a mean-product matrix. These matrices provide the
information used to estimate the variance and covariance components.

<<M60-4,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-maize-met", package = "SelectionTools")
m.maize.three <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(yld, dmc, pht) ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + env + gen:env + rep:env,
    data = maize.met,
    method = "moments",
    random.test = "F",
    control = list(design = "general")
)
m.maize.three
@

\begin{Soutput}
Multivariate linear mixed model fitted by the method of moments

Model:
  Data:      maize.met
  Fixed:     cbind(yld, dmc, pht) ~ 1
  Random:    ~gen + env + gen:env + rep:env
  Residual:  ~units

Design: general balanced orthogonal

ANOVA strata:
  Source     DF
  gen        24
  env        23
  gen:env   552
  env:rep    24
  Residual  576

Random-effect F tests by trait (term-specific tests):
  Source   Trait    DF1    DF2           SS          MS        F        p
  gen      yld       24    552   41507.1168   1729.4632  25.4559  <0.0001
  gen      dmc       24    552    3174.7852    132.2827  26.6975  <0.0001
  gen      pht       24    552  163618.6250   6817.4427  66.5983  <0.0001
  env      yld    23.23  41.15  202635.2081   8810.2264  31.0079  <0.0001
  env      dmc    23.14  41.44   23058.9308   1002.5622  48.8285  <0.0001
  env      pht    23.15  48.32  471250.9167  20489.1703  59.8423  <0.0001
  gen:env  yld      552    576   37502.6863     67.9396   1.5790  <0.0001
  gen:env  dmc      552    576    2735.0886      4.9549   1.6418  <0.0001
  gen:env  pht      552    576   56506.3750    102.3666   1.5471  <0.0001
  env:rep  yld       24    576    5221.8438    217.5768   5.0569  <0.0001
  env:rep  dmc       24    576     375.3424     15.6393   5.1820  <0.0001
  env:rep  pht       24    576    5787.0000    241.1250   3.6441  <0.0001

Test methods:
  gen: exact F
  env: quasi F (Satterthwaite)
  gen:env: exact F
  env:rep: exact F
  env [yld]: approximate
  env [dmc]: approximate
  env [pht]: approximate

Covariance components:
  gen:
           yld      dmc       pht
  yld  34.6151  -8.7208   35.6158
  dmc  -8.7208   2.6527   -6.7633
  pht  35.6158  -6.7633  139.8974

  env:
            yld      dmc       pht
  yld  171.3547  18.3650  168.6739
  dmc   18.3650  19.6997   35.9687
  pht  168.6739  35.9687  404.2369

  gen:env:
           yld     dmc      pht
  yld  12.4569  0.2046   5.4491
  dmc   0.2046  0.9684   0.5616
  pht   5.4491  0.5616  18.0991

  [1 covariance block omitted]

  Residual:
           yld      dmc      pht
  yld  43.0258   2.5554  15.4257
  dmc   2.5554   3.0180  -0.2425
  pht  15.4257  -0.2425  66.1684

  Diagonal entries are variances; off-diagonal entries are covariances.

Fit and model statistics:

  Trait: yld
  Observations  1200    Residual SD  6.5594
  Residual DF    576

  Trait: dmc
  Observations  1200    Residual SD  1.7372
  Residual DF    576

  Trait: pht
  Observations  1200    Residual SD  8.1344
  Residual DF    576
\end{Soutput}

The argument \cde{random.test = "F"} requests an F test for each random
effect and trait. The genotype test uses the genotype-by-environment
mean square as its denominator. The genotype-by-environment and
replicate-within-environment tests use the residual mean square. The
environment test combines mean squares from several strata and uses
Satterthwaite DF.

The \cde{control} list entry \cde{print.mean.products = TRUE} adds the
mean-product matrices to the model summary.

The covariance block for \cde{gen} describes how the random genotype
effects vary together across traits.

<<M60-5,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lmm.components(m.maize.three, "covariance", block = "gen")
@

\begin{Soutput}
           yld      dmc       pht
  yld  34.6151  -8.7208   35.6158
  dmc  -8.7208   2.6527   -6.7633
  pht  35.6158  -6.7633  139.8974
\end{Soutput}

The genetic correlations put these associations on a common scale.

<<M60-6,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lmm.components(m.maize.three, "correlation", block = "gen")
@

\begin{Soutput}
           yld      dmc      pht
  yld   1.0000  -0.9101   0.5118
  dmc  -0.9101   1.0000  -0.3511
  pht   0.5118  -0.3511   1.0000
\end{Soutput}

\subsubsection{Multivariate maize: Adjusted genotype means}

The model treats genotype as fixed and accounts for environments,
genotype-by-environment effects and replicates within environments
as random effects. The method of moments estimates covariances between
traits under the default residual formula. For this complete balanced
trial, the 75 least-squares mean estimates agree with the REML results
in Section~\ref{sec:maize-adjusted-means}.

<<maize-moments-means,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-maize-met", package = "SelectionTools")
m.maize.moments.means <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(yld, dmc, pht) ~ gen,
    random = ~env + gen:env + rep:env,
    data = maize.met,
    method = "moments",
    control = list(design = "general")
)
maize.moments.lsmeans <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(m.maize.moments.means, ~gen))
maize.moments.means <- xtabs(lsmeans ~ gen + trait,
    data = maize.moments.lsmeans)[, c("yld", "dmc", "pht")]
maize.moments.means
@

\begin{Soutput}
     trait
gen         yld       dmc       pht
  G01  55.94583  60.38292 167.29167
  G02  63.92854  58.28313 177.08333
  G03  65.31937  58.20021 159.06250
  G04  57.96688  61.20521 168.22917
  G05  56.48250  61.41000 166.66667
  G06  71.13375  56.76271 177.70833
  G07  76.85604  56.31000 177.18750
