\subsection{Adjusted genotype means for three traits}
\label{sec:maize-adjusted-means}

In the maize trial, grain yield, dry-matter content and plant height were
recorded on the same 1200 plots. A three-trait REML model treats genotype
as fixed. The random terms account for environments, genotype-by-environment
effects and replicates within environments. The residual covariance
allows the three measurements from the same plot to be correlated.

The least-squares means give one adjusted mean per genotype and trait.
The table places the three trait means side by side for all 25 genotypes.
Column indexing sets the trait order to \cde{yld}, \cde{dmc}, and
\cde{pht}.

<<maize-joint-means,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-maize-met", package = "SelectionTools")
m.maize.means <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(yld, dmc, pht) ~ gen,
    random = ~env + gen:env + rep:env,
    data = maize.met
)
maize.lsmeans <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(m.maize.means, ~gen))
maize.means <- xtabs(lsmeans ~ gen + trait,
    data = maize.lsmeans)[, c("yld", "dmc", "pht")]
maize.means
@

\begin{Soutput}
     trait
gen         yld       dmc       pht
