pht  0.5055 -0.3409  1.0000
\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Genetic variances and correlations in maize}
\label{sec:maize-genetic-reml}

In a second three-trait REML model, genotype is random. The diagonal
of the genotype covariance matrix gives the genetic variance for each
trait, and the off-diagonal entries give the genetic covariances.
Section~\ref{sec:multivariate-maize} presents the moment estimates for
the same trial.

<<maize-genetic-reml,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-maize-met", package = "SelectionTools")
m.maize.genetic <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(yld, dmc, pht) ~ 1,
    random = ~gen + env + gen:env + rep:env,
    data = maize.met
)
st.lmm.components(m.maize.genetic, "covariance", block = "gen")
@

\begin{Soutput}
           yld      dmc       pht
  yld  34.6151  -8.7208   35.6158
  dmc  -8.7208   2.6527   -6.7633
  pht  35.6158  -6.7633  139.8974
\end{Soutput}

The genetic correlations describe the associations on a common scale.

<<maize-genetic-cor,eval=FALSE>>=
st.lmm.components(m.maize.genetic, "correlation", block = "gen")
@

\begin{Soutput}
           yld      dmc      pht
  yld   1.0000  -0.9101   0.5118
  dmc  -0.9101   1.0000  -0.3511
  pht   0.5118  -0.3511   1.0000
\end{Soutput}

Table~\ref{tab:maize-fit-timing} compares the three-trait fits on the
1200-plot maize trial. Times cover the \cde{st.lmm()} call, including its
default tests. Peak memory is the resident memory of a fresh R process.
The reference BLAS figures come from one run with one SelectionTools
thread. The OpenBLAS figures are medians of three runs with four threads
each for OpenBLAS and SelectionTools.

