A second fit treats genotype as fixed and retains random genotype-by-
environment effects. The least-squares means use the same reference
grid of environments and replicate blocks for every genotype. The
model estimates an expected value for combinations without retained
plots. The table gives one adjusted mean per genotype and trait.

<<M47-lsmeans,eval=FALSE>>=
m.met.seven.means <- st.lmm(
    fixed = cbind(YLD, HLW, TKW, PLH, DH, DM, SC) ~ gen + env + rep:env,
    random = ~gen:env,
    cov = ~at(env):units,
    data = barley.seven.traits,
    random.test = "none",
    control = list(backend = "block")
)
barley.lsmeans <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(m.met.seven.means, ~gen))
round(xtabs(lsmeans ~ gen + trait, data = barley.lsmeans)[
    , c("YLD", "HLW", "TKW", "PLH", "DH", "DM", "SC")
], 2)
@

