\subsubsection{Implemented features and limitations}

\noindent\textbf{Implemented features.}\par\smallskip
\begin{itemize}
\item Gaussian ML and REML random-coefficient models with random intercepts,
slopes and expanded coefficient bases, as in \cde{random=\textasciitilde Time | Chick}.
An unmodified coefficient block estimates its full covariance.
\cde{diag()} or the double-bar form specifies independent expanded coefficients.
\item Univariate coefficient covariance families \cde{us()}, \cde{diag()},
\cde{homdiag()}, compound symmetry and AR1, with the documented homogeneous
or heterogeneous scales. Named relationships can connect grouping levels;
other supported random and residual terms can accompany the regression.
\item Time-specific residual variances, fitted-trajectory predictions,
refits, updates and parametric simulation through their supported interfaces.
Satterthwaite and asymptotic inference are available when the covariance
information is usable; covariance and boundary diagnostics are retained.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Limitations.}\par\smallskip
\begin{itemize}
\item AR1 on random coefficients uses their positions in the expanded design
basis. It does not automatically model elapsed-time distances or within-subject
residual correlation. Factor coding, intercept inclusion and coefficient order
therefore define the covariance model; residual correlation requires its own
\cde{cov} specification.
\item The structured coefficient modifiers described here apply to univariate
likelihood fits, not moment estimation. Multivariate random regressions use
trait-by-coefficient covariance and do not inherit every univariate modifier.
\item Kenward--Roger adjustment is available only for supported linear
covariance families. Heterogeneous compound symmetry and AR1 with more than
two coefficients reject it; Satterthwaite and asymptotic inference are
supported alternatives. A zero or singular covariance block does not certify ordinary
regular inference, and local boundary checks do not prove a global optimum.
\item The genetic-level \cde{st.predict} interface does not select random-slope
blocks; conditional \cde{predict} returns trajectories. Whole-term
random-effect tests delete the coefficient block together, rather than
performing automatic stepwise slope selection. Extrapolation beyond observed
times remains a model assumption, not an automatically validated forecast.
\end{itemize}

\section{Diallel analysis}
\label{sec:breeding-extensions}

\subsection{Half-diallel example}

This example fits a half-diallel model to maize sugar measurements.
Shared parental effects describe general combining ability (GCA), while
unordered cross effects describe specific combining ability (SCA).

\label{sec:half-diallel}

The maize dataset contains sugar measurements for 21 crosses among seven
parents, with two technical repetitions per cross. Its 42 observations
retain the source response values and repetition labels. The data are used in
the \cde{lme4breeding} quantitative-genetics vignette and distributed with
\cde{enhancer} (\litref{covarrubias2016}{Covarrubias-Pazaran, 2016}).

The packaged data already contain parental factors with the same seven
levels P1 through P7 and an unordered cross factor labelled P1:P2,
for example, for SCA. The redundant source identifier \cde{geno} is
omitted; \cde{cross} identifies the cross and supplies its SCA term.
The following call loads these data directly.

<<E01-data,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-lmm-halfdiallel", package = "SelectionTools")
head(halfdiallel, 5)
@

\begin{Soutput}
  rep male female cross     sugar
1   1   P1     P2 P1:P2 13.950509
2   2   P1     P2 P1:P2  9.756918
3   1   P1     P3 P1:P3 13.906355
4   2   P1     P3 P1:P3  9.119455
5   1   P1     P4 P1:P4  5.174483
\end{Soutput}




The \cde{overlay(female, male)} term uses one shared parental effect
vector and one GCA variance. Each cross receives the sum of its two
parental effects; a self cross would have loading two. The separate cross
term models SCA, following \litref{sprague1942}{Sprague and Tatum (1942)}.
The fixed model contains only an intercept, matching the
\cde{lme4breeding} half-diallel example. The technical-repetition labels
are retained in the data but are not fitted as a blocking factor.

The fitted model's variance-component table reports the shared
parental GCA, cross SCA and residual variances directly.

<<E01-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
m.diallel <- st.lmm(
    fixed = sugar ~ 1,
    random = ~overlay(female, male) + cross,
    data = halfdiallel
)
m.diallel
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      halfdiallel
  Fixed:     sugar ~ 1
  Random:    ~overlay(female, male) + cross
  Residual:  ~units

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term                   DF       LR       p
  overlay(female, male)   1  11.4690  0.0007
  cross                   1   2.5752  0.1086

Variance components:
  Component              Variance
  overlay(female, male)    5.5088
  cross                    1.8160
  Residual                 3.1174

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                42    R-squared (conditional)   0.8046
  Log-likelihood        -97.7232    RMSE (conditional)        1.4356
  AIC                   203.4464    Variance: fixed           0.0000
  BIC                   210.3971    Variance: random         12.8335
  R-squared (marginal)    0.0000    Variance: residual        3.1174
\end{Soutput}


These estimates show close numerical agreement with the published
\cde{lme4breeding} example: GCA 5.5088, SCA 1.8160 and residual variance
3.1173. This is a comparison with its displayed output, rather than a
fresh run of that package. A known parental relationship can be supplied
with \cde{ucov=list(overlay=A)}, with both axes named by parent identifier.
Separate female and male random terms would not impose the shared-parent
model. Reciprocal and maternal effects require separate specification.
The current overlay is univariate and stores a dense parent basis.

Predict each parent's GCA effect without the intercept, with prediction-error
standard errors and pointwise intervals.

<<E01-parent,eval=FALSE>>=
diallel.parents <- st.predict(
    m.diallel,
    "0 + overlay(female, male)"
)
diallel.parents
@

\begin{Soutput}
Shared parental effects from overlay(female, male)
Predicted random effects
  Parent  prediction      SE   DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  P2          0.9190  1.1412  Inf   -1.3177    3.1557
  P3          2.8773  1.1412  Inf    0.6406    5.1140
  P4         -3.8482  1.1412  Inf   -6.0849   -1.6115
  P5          0.6570  1.1412  Inf   -1.5797    2.8937
  P6          1.6020  1.1412  Inf   -0.6347    3.8387
  P7         -1.3027  1.1412  Inf   -3.5394    0.9340
  P1         -0.9043  1.1412  Inf   -3.1410    1.3324
Confidence level: 0.9500
SE and intervals use prediction-error variance; fitted variances held
  fixed.
\end{Soutput}


The \cde{0 +} prefix requests GCA effects alone. Omitting it returns
the fixed intercept plus GCA.
Their joint prediction-error covariance is returned by
\cde{vcov(diallel.parents)}.

Predict SCA effects alone, then complete cross means. The latter combine
the intercept, both shared parental effects and SCA. Each distinct target
is reported once; standard errors include their joint covariance.

<<E01-sca,eval=FALSE>>=
st.predict(m.diallel, "0 + cross")
@

