\section{Analysis of a series of trials}
\label{sec:series}

\subsection{Tropical maize: A series across environments}

This example analyses tropical-maize lattice trials in stages. Each environment first has
its own ordinary mixed model, with fixed genotype effects and random
replicates and incomplete blocks within replicates. The series analysis
then uses the adjusted genotype means and their full covariance matrices.
This follows the fully efficient stagewise approach of
\litref{piepho2012}{Piepho et al. (2012)} and
\litref{endelman2023}{Endelman (2023)}.

The package, plot data and marker data are loaded independently of the
other examples. The first three environment labels select three independent
trials; all their observed phenotypes are used.

<<tropmaize-series-setup,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
st.set.num.threads(1)
data("v-lmm-tropmaize-lattice", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-tropmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.tropmaize.vcf, data.set = "maize.series")
envs <- unique(tropmaize.lattice$env)
G <- st.get.A("maize.series")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.series'): New data set generated 'maize.series'
M (data set 'maize.series'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}


Each environment is fitted separately. Genotypes remain fixed so that these
adjusted observations have not already undergone genetic shrinkage.

<<tropmaize-series-env1,eval=FALSE>>=
fit.env1 <- st.lmm(y ~ gen, random = ~ rep + rep:block,
    data = droplevels(subset(tropmaize.lattice, env == envs[1])))
@

<<tropmaize-series-env2,eval=FALSE>>=
fit.env2 <- st.lmm(y ~ gen, random = ~ rep + rep:block,
    data = droplevels(subset(tropmaize.lattice, env == envs[2])))
@

<<tropmaize-series-env3,eval=FALSE>>=
fit.env3 <- st.lmm(y ~ gen, random = ~ rep + rep:block,
    data = droplevels(subset(tropmaize.lattice, env == envs[3])))
@

The next \cde{st.lmm} call receives the fitted models. The first entry
of \cde{series} names the environment factor; its labels are recovered
from the earlier fits. The common fixed genotype factor identifies the
means to extract. The genomic matrix supplies relationships for the
random main genotype effect, while \cde{gen:env} specifies independent
additional genotype-by-environment variation.

<<tropmaize-series-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
fit.series.G <- st.lmm(y ~ env,
    random = ~ gen + gen:env,
    ucov = list(gen = G),
    series = list("env", fit.env1, fit.env2, fit.env3))
fit.series.G
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      series of earlier fits
  Fixed:     y ~ env
  Random:    ~gen + gen:env
  Residual: supplied covariance; no extra units variance
  Covariance: gen (264 levels; supplied matrix)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Fixed effects (Satterthwaite):
  Term  DF1      DF2        F        p
  env     2  4219.23  13.4339  <0.0001

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term     DF         LR        p
  gen       1  1423.0027  <0.0001
  gen:env   1   901.6339  <0.0001

Variance components:
  Stage-1 estimation covariance: full; scale fixed at 1
  This covariance is retained, not estimated in this fit.
  Component  Variance
  gen          1.0311
  gen:env      0.0287

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                792    R-squared (conditional)  0.9947
  Log-likelihood        -418.7741    RMSE (conditional)       0.0389
  AIC                    847.5481    Variance: fixed          0.0058
  BIC                    870.9209    Variance: random         1.8074
  R-squared (marginal)     0.0032    Variance: residual       0.0096
\end{Soutput}

\paragraph{Stage-one model and adjusted observations.}
For independent trial $e$, write the plot-level model as
\[
 y_e=X_e\beta_e+Z_e b_e+\epsilon_e,\qquad
 R_e=Z_e B_e Z_e^\mathsf{T}+R_{\epsilon,e}.
\]
Genotypes are fixed in $\beta_e$; replicates and incomplete blocks are
trial-specific random effects. Each trial estimates its own covariance
components, so differences in plot error variance between environments
are retained. Conditional on these components, the adjusted genotype
means and their estimation-error covariance are
\[
 \widehat m_e=L_e\widehat\beta_e,\qquad
 C_e=L_e(X_e^\mathsf{T}R_e^{-1}X_e)^{-}L_e^\mathsf{T}.
\]
Here $L_e$ defines estimable genotype means, and the superscript $-$
denotes a generalized inverse when needed. The series interface uses the
marginal-means reference grid: nuisance factors are averaged
equally and numeric covariates use their default reference values.
Only genotype means are forwarded; covariate slopes and their joint
covariance are not transferred by this interface. First-stage genotype
BLUPs are rejected because they have already undergone shrinkage.

\paragraph{Full covariance propagation and stage-two model.}
Stack the means and their covariances in the same trial and genotype
order. Independence of the earlier analyses gives
\[
 \widehat m=(\widehat m_1^\mathsf{T},\ldots,
              \widehat m_E^\mathsf{T})^\mathsf{T},\qquad
 C=C_1\oplus\cdots\oplus C_E.
\]
Off-diagonal entries within each $C_e$ are retained. They reflect shared
block adjustments and other dependence between the estimated means;
independent trials have zero cross-trial blocks. The second-stage model is
\[
 \widehat m=X\alpha+Z_g a+Z_{ge}u+\delta,\qquad
 a\sim N(0,\sigma_a^2G),\quad
 u\sim N(0,\sigma_{ge}^2I),\quad
 \delta\sim N(0,C),
\]
with mutually independent random contributions. $X\alpha$ contains fixed
environment means, $a$ is the genomic genotype main effect, and $u$ is
additional genotype-by-environment variation. Thus
\[
 V=C+\sigma_a^2 Z_gGZ_g^\mathsf{T}
       +\sigma_{ge}^2 Z_{ge}Z_{ge}^\mathsf{T}.
\]
There is one adjusted observation per retained genotype--environment
combination, so $Z_{ge}Z_{ge}^\mathsf{T}=I$ in this example.
The complete $C$ is supplied on its absolute response scale with
coefficient fixed at one. Stage two estimates genetic and G$\times$E
components, not a new multiplier for the first-stage error. Explicit
\cde{gen:env} therefore suppresses an additional units variance: with
one observation per combination it would duplicate the same independent
covariance component. The printed inherited covariance describes a
fixed contribution, not an estimated variance of one for every mean.

\paragraph{Weighting, estimation and prediction.}
The precision of the means is used through the full mixed-model
covariance $V$. With $K=\operatorname{Var}[(a^\mathsf{T},u^\mathsf{T})^
\mathsf{T}]$ and $Z=(Z_g,Z_{ge})$,
\[
 \widehat\alpha=(X^\mathsf{T}V^{-1}X)^{-1}
                  X^\mathsf{T}V^{-1}\widehat m,\qquad
 \widehat b=KZ^\mathsf{T}P\widehat m,
\]
\[
 P=V^{-1}-V^{-1}X(X^\mathsf{T}V^{-1}X)^{-1}X^\mathsf{T}V^{-1},
 \qquad \operatorname{PEV}(b)=K-KZ^\mathsf{T}PZK.
\]
These expressions assume an estimable full-rank fixed parameterization;
the implementation handles aliases through its estimability machinery.
No separate location weights are needed. In a simple trial with
independent replicated observations, a mean has error variance
$\sigma_e^2/r_{ie}$, not $\sigma_e^2$. In an incomplete-block analysis,
$C_e$ additionally carries unequal precisions and correlations.
Replacing $C_e$ by its diagonal is an approximation; replacing all
blocks by an average error variance discards still more information.
The across-environment prediction averages fixed environment
means equally and adds the genotype BLUP. This defines the prediction
target; equal target weights do not imply equal precision in fitting.
Prediction-error SEs include uncertainty in that complete target,
including fixed--random covariance. Genetic reliability concerns only
$a$ and, conditional on covariance components, is
$1-\operatorname{PEV}(a_i)/\operatorname{Var}(a_i)$.

\subsection{Barley: Stagewise analysis across years and locations}
\label{sec:barley-series}

This independent example uses thousand-kernel weight (\cde{TKW}) in the
packaged barley multi-environment trial: 15 genotypes, five locations,
three years and four replicates per location--year trial. Locations recur
across years, so persistent genotype-by-location effects can be separated
from genotype-by-year and genotype-by-location-by-year effects. No genomic
relationship matrix is supplied for these data; genotype effects are
independent. The preceding covariance-propagation theory applies unchanged.

Give each location--year trial a unique environment label.

<<barley-series-setup,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-barley-met", package = "SelectionTools")
barley.met$env <- interaction(barley.met$loc, barley.met$year,
    drop = TRUE)
@


The 15 trials are fitted separately with fixed genotypes and random replicates.
The list retains its environment names. Each trial's data also retains
\cde{loc} and factor \cde{year}; they are constant within that trial and
are carried into the second stage. Genotype and replicate levels unused
in an individual trial are dropped.

<<barley-series-first,eval=FALSE>>=
barley.trials <- split(barley.met, barley.met$env)
barley.first <- lapply(barley.trials, function(trial) {
    st.lmm(TKW ~ gen, random = ~ rep,
        data = droplevels(trial))
})
@


The ordinary fitted models enter the combined analysis directly. Fixed \cde{env} gives each
location--year combination its own mean. The four random terms represent
an overall genotype effect, persistent genotype-by-location effects,
genotype-by-year effects shared across locations, and transient
genotype-by-location-by-year effects, respectively.

<<barley-series-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
barley.series <- st.lmm(TKW ~ env,
    random = ~ gen + gen:loc + gen:year + gen:env,
    series = c(list("env"), barley.first))
barley.series
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      series of earlier fits
  Fixed:     TKW ~ env
  Random:    ~gen + gen:loc + gen:year + gen:env
  Residual: supplied covariance; no extra units variance

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Fixed effects (Satterthwaite):
  Term  DF1     DF2        F        p
  env    14  101.72  26.8215  <0.0001

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term      DF        LR        p
  gen        1    7.4767   0.0063
  gen:loc    1   28.7302  <0.0001
  gen:year   1   96.6672  <0.0001
  gen:env    1  118.2846  <0.0001

Variance components:
  Stage-1 estimation covariance: full; scale fixed at 1
  This covariance is retained, not estimated in this fit.
  Component  Variance
  gen         12.1324
  gen:loc      4.3073
  gen:year    13.1456
  gen:env      3.8547

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                225    R-squared (conditional)   0.9668
  Log-likelihood        -597.7058    RMSE (conditional)        0.6634
  AIC                   1233.4116    Variance: fixed          31.0305
  BIC                   1298.3175    Variance: random         33.4400
  R-squared (marginal)     0.4653    Variance: residual        2.2124
\end{Soutput}

Predict the overall genotype values averaged equally over the 15 trial
means. These are predictions for the observed location--year series;
they are not predictions for a future year. The SE includes the fixed
mean and genetic prediction-error covariance, whereas reliability refers
to the genotype main effect. Both condition on the fitted variance components.

<<barley-series-predict,eval=FALSE>>=
barley.prediction <- st.predict(barley.series, "gen")
barley.prediction
@

\begin{Soutput}
Predicted means: average environment + genetic effect
  gen    prediction      SE   DF  lower.CL  upper.CL  reliability
  lne01     36.8501  1.9936  Inf   32.9427   40.7574       0.6376
  lne02     44.1093  1.9936  Inf   40.2019   48.0166       0.6376
  lne03     45.8882  1.9936  Inf   41.9808   49.7955       0.6376
  lne04     40.9527  1.9936  Inf   37.0454   44.8601       0.6376
  lne05     43.6698  1.9936  Inf   39.7624   47.5771       0.6376
  lne06     42.1637  1.9936  Inf   38.2564   46.0711       0.6376
  lne07     42.0965  1.9936  Inf   38.1892   46.0039       0.6376
  lne08     42.1406  1.9936  Inf   38.2333   46.0480       0.6376
  lne09     43.8823  1.9936  Inf   39.9749   47.7896       0.6376
  lne10     38.4597  1.9936  Inf   34.5524   42.3671       0.6376
  lne11     43.8333  1.9936  Inf   39.9260   47.7407       0.6376
  lne12     44.4298  1.9936  Inf   40.5225   48.3372       0.6376
  lne13     37.7532  1.9936  Inf   33.8458   41.6605       0.6376
  lne14     44.7179  1.9936  Inf   40.8106   48.6253       0.6376
  lne15     46.3324  1.9936  Inf   42.4250   50.2397       0.6376
Confidence level: 0.9500
SE and intervals use prediction-error variance; fitted variances held
  fixed.
\end{Soutput}

The inherited residual covariance is the block diagonal matrix of the
15 first-stage adjusted-mean covariance matrices, on their original scale.
Its multiplier is fixed at one. The \cde{gen:env} term supplies the
remaining transient genetic variation; an additional independent units
variance would be confounded with this term and is therefore omitted.
Each trial can have its own estimated replicate and plot-error variances.

This dataset has a complete location--year grid. The same construction
allows missing location--year combinations by fitting only observed
trials; estimation then depends on connectivity and adequate overlap.
Three years provide limited information about the genotype-by-year
variance. Boundary estimates should be interpreted in that context.


\section{Genome wide association studies}
\label{sec:gwas}

This example analyses rapeseed flowering time with genome-wide SNP
markers. The example covers kinship-adjusted
marker scans, principal-component covariates, a resampled genome-wide
significance threshold and a regional plot.

\subsection{Rapeseed marker and flowering-time data}

The data comprise 193 rapeseed lines with observed flowering time and
22,857 SNPs, provided by Rod Snowdon (University of Giessen).
All markers and lines are included in the package data.

The packaged STvcf object and phenotype table provide the inputs
for a SelectionTools marker data set.

<<E03-load,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-rapeseed", package = "SelectionTools")
st.set.num.threads(1)
st.load.vcf.data(v.rapeseed.vcf,
                 data.set = "rapeseed")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'rapeseed'): New data set generated 'rapeseed'
M (data set 'rapeseed'): No. of individuals: 193, no. of markers: 22857
\end{Soutput}




The physical map is imported with the STvcf object and retained in the
marker data set. Association scans use these stored chromosome labels
and base-pair positions automatically.

<<E03-phenotypes,eval=FALSE>>=
head(v.rapeseed.phe)
@

\begin{Soutput}
    gen   flt
1 SW001 154.5
2 SW002 157.0
3 SW003 154.0
4 SW004 156.0
5 SW005 155.5
6 SW006 152.5
\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Kinship-adjusted GWAS with P3D}

Each marker model has an additive fixed marker effect, an
intercept and a random polygenic effect with covariance proportional to
the genomic relationship matrix. This accounts for relatedness among
lines, following the mixed-model association framework of
\litref{yu2006}{Yu et al. (2006)}. With \cde{P3D=TRUE}, the null-model
covariance is estimated once and its shape is held fixed during the
marker scan. This is the variance-reuse strategy underlying EMMAX and
P3D (\litref{kang2010}{Kang et al., 2010};
\litref{zhang2010}{Zhang et al., 2010}).



<<E03-k-p3d,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.k <- st.gwas(
    fixed = flt ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    data = v.rapeseed.phe,
    marker.data.set = "rapeseed",
    K = "A",
    min.MAF = 0.05,
    P3D = TRUE)
print(rapeseed.k, top = 3, bottom = 0)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Genome-wide additive marker tests
F test; P3D: TRUE
  Trait  Marker     MAF   Effect      SE       p  Status
  flt    Q02002  0.0751  -0.7318  0.7182  0.3095  ok    
  flt    Q02016  0.2202   0.6240  0.4838  0.1987  ok    
  flt    Q02023  0.1839   0.6280  0.5165  0.2256  ok    
  [22854 table rows omitted]
\end{Soutput}


Symbolic \cde{K = "A"} constructs the genomic relationship from the
complete marker panel. The marker tests retain lines with observed
flowering time and require a minor allele frequency of at least 0.05
among those lines. The output table keeps filtered markers with their
status. For the ordinary residual model used here, SelectionTools
reports a one-degree-of-freedom marker $F$ test with marker-model residual
degrees of freedom and a re-estimated residual scale.

The \cde{plot} method draws the Manhattan plot directly from the fitted
\cde{st.gwas} object. Chromosomes and marker positions are read from that
object. Setting \cde{threshold=FALSE} suppresses a cutoff line.

<<E03-figure-k-manhattan,eval=FALSE>>=
plot(rapeseed.k, which = "manhattan",
     main = "Flowering time: K model", threshold = FALSE,
     cex = 0.42)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:gwas-k-manhattan} shows the Manhattan plot for the K model.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\phasefigure{figures/gwas-rapeseed-k-manhattan.png}{0.96}
\caption{Genome-wide association scores for flowering time under the
kinship model with P3D and without principal-component covariates. Chromosome labels
and positions come from the supplied physical map.}
\label{fig:gwas-k-manhattan}
\end{figure}

The QQ diagnostic for this K model is shown with the PK model below.

\subsection{Re-estimating variance components for every marker}

A second scan uses \cde{P3D=FALSE}. Free variance components are now
re-estimated with each marker included in the fixed model. This
illustrates the marker-specific variance-estimation strategy used in
EMMA (\litref{kang2008}{Kang et al. (2008)}).

<<E03-k-reml,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.k.reml <- st.gwas(
    fixed = flt ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    data = v.rapeseed.phe,
    marker.data.set = "rapeseed",
    K = "A",
    P3D = FALSE)
print(rapeseed.k.reml, top = 3, bottom = 0)
@

\begin{Soutput}
Genome-wide additive marker tests
F test; P3D: FALSE
  Trait  Marker     MAF   Effect      SE       p  Status
  flt    Q02002  0.0751  -0.7318  0.7182  0.3095  ok    
  flt    Q02016  0.2202   0.6256  0.4833  0.1971  ok    
  flt    Q02023  0.1839   0.6343  0.5156  0.2201  ok    
  [22854 table rows omitted]
\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Kinship and principal-component adjustment}

Symbolic \cde{K = "A"} constructs the relationship matrix from the full
marker panel. For these biallelic genotypes, the marker dose $d_{ij}$
counts 0, 1 or 2 copies of the chosen allele at marker $j$ in line $i$.
SelectionTools retains the two allele calls and derives doses for the
calculations. With allele frequency $p_j$, centring and scaling give
\[
 Z_{ij}=d_{ij}-2p_j,\qquad
 K=\frac{ZZ^\mathsf{T}}{2\sum_j p_j(1-p_j)}.
\]
Allele frequencies are calculated from observed genotypes in the complete
marker panel. The sum covers polymorphic biallelic markers; missing doses
are replaced by $2p_j$ for this calculation, giving a centred score of zero.
Scaling uses one denominator for the matrix, following
\litref{vanraden2008}{VanRaden (2008)} and
\litref{endelman2012}{Endelman and Jannink (2012)}.



The next scan retains that matrix and adds three principal-component covariates.
These covariates are
unit eigenvectors of the relationship matrix, calculated internally.

<<E03-pk-three,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.pk3 <- st.gwas(
    fixed = flt ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    data = v.rapeseed.phe,
    marker.data.set = "rapeseed",
    K = "A",
    n.PC = 3,
    P3D = TRUE)
@

The comparison counts successful marker tests and reports FDR decisions
at 0.05 using the explicit adjustment and significance-level options below.
Model labels use the fitted object names. When significant markers occur
on several chromosomes, the summary reports the lead marker on each chromosome.

<<E03-model-summary,eval=FALSE>>=
compare.gwas.fits(
    rapeseed.k,
    rapeseed.pk3,
    adjust = "FDR",
    alpha = 0.05
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
GWAS summary: FDR; alpha = 0.05
  Model         Trait  PCs  Tested  Significant
  rapeseed.k    flt      0   22836            1
  rapeseed.pk3  flt      3   22836            1

Chromosome leads (overall lead when none are significant)

Model: rapeseed.k; trait: flt
  Marker  Chr  Position     MAF  Effect        p     FDR
  Q46054  15   41874539  0.1503  3.0980  <0.0001  0.0070

Model: rapeseed.pk3; trait: flt
  Marker  Chr  Position     MAF  Effect        p     FDR
  Q46054  15   41874539  0.1503  3.2351  <0.0001  0.0034
\end{Soutput}


Both models identify Q46054 as their leading association. FDR decisions
refer to the complete marker scan for each model and trait.

<<E03-figure-pk-qq,eval=FALSE>>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4.5, 4.5, 2.2, 0.7),
    mgp = c(2.7, 0.8, 0), cex.axis = 0.85)
plot(rapeseed.k, which = "qq", main = "K", level = 0.95,
     col = "cornflowerblue", cex = 0.38)
plot(rapeseed.pk3, which = "qq", main = "PK: 3 PCs", level = 0.95,
     col = "cornflowerblue", cex = 0.38)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:gwas-qq} compares the K model with the model including three PCs.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\phasefigure{figures/gwas-rapeseed-pk-qq.png}{0.98}
\caption{QQ comparison of the K model and the PK model with three
principal-component covariates. The envelopes are pointwise diagnostic
references.}\label{fig:gwas-qq}
\end{figure}

The following table shows the leading markers from the model with three
principal components.

<<E03-hits,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.summary <- summarize.gwas.fit(
    rapeseed.pk3,
    adjust = "FDR",
    alpha = 0.05
)
rapeseed.summary
@

\begin{Soutput}
GWAS summary: FDR; alpha = 0.05
  Model         Trait  PCs  Tested  Significant
  rapeseed.pk3  flt      3   22836            1

Leading markers

Model: rapeseed.pk3; trait: flt
  Marker  Chr  Position     MAF   Effect        p     FDR
  Q46054  15   41874539  0.1503   3.2351  <0.0001  0.0034
  Q02704  2     7109895  0.0570  -3.1822  <0.0001  0.7338
  Q04021  5    22302146  0.0751   2.9345   0.0001  0.7338
  Q04467  2     2767764  0.2746  -1.6811   0.0002  0.7338
  Q02720  12   13080275  0.1347  -2.1281   0.0002  0.7338
  Q04468  2     2768143  0.2772  -1.6762   0.0002  0.7338
\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Resampled genome-wide significance threshold}

A genome-wide cutoff for the model with three principal
components is calibrated by simulating phenotypes under its fitted marker-free
null model. The marker panel, relationship matrix, principal components,
fixed covariates and marker filters are retained.
Each simulated phenotype has newly generated polygenic and residual
effects. This preserves the fitted covariance between related lines,
as required for mixed-model null simulation
(\litref{joo2016}{Joo et al., 2016}).

A nonempty \cde{sim.threshold} list enables the calculation. Its options
are \cde{nsim}, \cde{alpha}, \cde{seed} and \cde{method}; omitted options default to
999 simulations, level 0.05 and the current random-number stream.
The default empty \cde{list()} performs no simulation. The explicit
seed below makes the calculation reproducible while preserving the
caller's random-number state.

Here \cde{method = "parametricFixedVC"} holds the original fitted variance
components fixed across simulated scans, retaining a separate residual
scale for each marker's $F$ test. This is a conditional, plug-in calibration.
The alternative \cde{method = "parametric"} refits the null model for each simulated response.
With \cde{P3D = TRUE}, each replicate reuses its own fitted covariance shape across markers.
With \cde{P3D = FALSE}, variance components are also refitted at each marker.

<<E03-simulated-threshold,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.pk3.sim <- st.gwas(
    fixed = flt ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    data = v.rapeseed.phe,
    marker.data.set = "rapeseed",
    K = "A",
    n.PC = 3,
    P3D = TRUE,
    control = list(batch.size = 1024L),
    sim.threshold = list(method = "parametricFixedVC",
                         nsim = 999L, alpha = 0.05, seed = 1L))
rapeseed.pk3.sim
@

\begin{Soutput}
Genome-wide additive marker tests
F test; P3D: TRUE
  Trait  Marker     MAF   Effect      SE       p   p.sim  Status
  flt    Q02002  0.0751  -0.7227  0.7285  0.3224  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02016  0.2202   0.5869  0.4849  0.2277  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02023  0.1839   0.5679  0.5182  0.2745  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02025  0.2591   0.4758  0.4516  0.2935  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02078  0.0803  -0.9455  0.9672  0.3296  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02079  0.2383  -0.1076  0.4725  0.8202  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02218  0.2280   0.2366  0.4817  0.6239  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02222  0.1321  -0.6579  0.5712  0.2509  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02233  0.3083  -0.0854  0.4373  0.8455  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q02247  0.2202   0.8041  0.4728  0.0907  1.0000  ok    
  [22842 table rows omitted]
  flt    Q23145  0.1606  -0.3432  0.6040  0.5705  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q22687  0.2824   0.3636  0.4271  0.3957  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q39875  0.0699   0.4304  0.7167  0.5488  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q39833  0.1166  -0.2814  0.6203  0.6507  1.0000  ok    
  flt    Q51873  0.0881  -0.0148  0.6696  0.9823  1.0000  ok    

Simulated genome-wide thresholds (parametric null; each trait separately)
Replicates: 999; alpha: 0.05; seed: 1
  Trait  Markers  Successful  Failed   Score        p  Status
  flt      22836         999       0  5.3205  <0.0001  ok    
Use p.sim <= alpha; score cutoffs require a strict exceedance.
\end{Soutput}

For replicate $b$, let $M_b$ be the largest association score
$-\log_{10}(p)$ among the eligible markers. For an observed marker score
$s$, the reported simulation-adjusted probability is
\[
  p_{\mathrm{sim}}(s) =
  \frac{1 + \#\{b:M_b \geq s\}}{B+1}.
\]
The additional count prevents a simulated probability of zero
(\litref{phipson2010}{Phipson and Smyth, 2010}). With $B=999$, the
smallest reported value is 0.001. The score cutoff at level $\alpha$
is the $k$th smallest maximum, where
$k=B+1-\lfloor\alpha(B+1)\rfloor$. For this run $k=950$.
A score must be strictly above the cutoff to pass; the count-based
\cde{p.sim} values determine decisions in the presence of ties.

<<E03-simulation-hits,eval=FALSE>>=
summarize.gwas.simulation(rapeseed.pk3.sim)
@

\begin{Soutput}
GWAS summary: simulation; alpha = 0.05
  Model             Trait  PCs  Tested  Significant
  rapeseed.pk3.sim  flt      3   22836            1

Leading markers

Model: rapeseed.pk3.sim; trait: flt
  Marker  Chr   Score        p   p.sim
  Q46054  15   6.8320  <0.0001  0.0010
  Q02704  2    4.0665  <0.0001  0.4900
  Q04021  5    3.8963   0.0001  0.6150
  Q04467  2    3.8013   0.0002  0.6970
  Q02720  12   3.7079   0.0002  0.7700
  Q04468  2    3.6147   0.0002  0.8310
\end{Soutput}


This calibration targets the probability of at least one false positive
across the eligible markers under the fitted complete-null model for
this trait. It depends on that model and its estimated covariance;
it does not jointly adjust the different traits or model choices.
All requested simulated scans must complete. Failed fits or eligible
marker tests are recorded in \cde{simulation\$diagnostics}, and an
incomplete calibration does not receive a threshold or adjusted
probabilities. The maxima are retained in \cde{simulation\$max.score}.

The Manhattan helper reads the saved threshold automatically. Its coral
dashed line is labelled with the level and score cutoff. Plotting only
uses saved results; it performs no further simulation. An explicit
\cde{threshold=FALSE}, numerical raw-$p$-value threshold, or \cde{adjust} setting
overrides the automatic choice.

<<E03-figure-pk-manhattan,eval=FALSE>>=
plot(rapeseed.pk3.sim, which = "manhattan", adjust = "simulation",
     main = "Flowering time: K + 3 PCs",
     cex = 0.42)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:gwas-pk-manhattan} shows the Manhattan plot with the
resampled genome-wide threshold.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\phasefigure{figures/gwas-rapeseed-pk-manhattan.png}{0.96}
\caption{Genome-wide association scores for flowering time after
adjustment for kinship and three principal components. The coral dashed
line gives the genome-wide 0.05 cutoff from 999 null simulations.}
\label{fig:gwas-pk-manhattan}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Regional association}

The regional plot around Q46054 on chromosome 15 highlights a 500-kb
interval and the nominal $p<10^{-5}$ cutoff.

<<E03-region,eval=FALSE>>=
rapeseed.lead <- rapeseed.summary$markers[1, ]
@

<<E03-figure-region,eval=FALSE>>=
plot(rapeseed.pk3, which = "region",
     chromosome = rapeseed.lead$chromosome,
     xlim = rapeseed.lead$position + c(-2e6, 2e6),
     position.scale = 1e6, position.unit = "Mb",
     shade = rapeseed.lead$position + c(-250000, 250000),
     highlight = rapeseed.lead$marker, labels = TRUE,
     threshold = 1e-5, main = "Flowering time: K + 3 PCs",
     col = "cornflowerblue", cex = 0.65)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:gwas-region} shows the regional Manhattan plot around Q46054.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\phasefigure{figures/gwas-rapeseed-region.png}{0.96}
\caption{Regional Manhattan plot around Q46054 on chromosome 15. The
shaded 500-kb interval contains 17 markers; the dashed line
and highlighted point identify the nominal $p<10^{-5}$ criterion.}
\label{fig:gwas-region}
\end{figure}


\section{Genomic prediction}

\subsection{Two-step genomic prediction with one dataset containing the unobserved phenotypic values of the prediction set as NA}

The first commands attach SelectionTools and load the plot data and
marker calls for the tropical maize example. The marker calls are stored in
\cde{"maize.lmm"}.

<<tropmaize-prediction-load,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-lmm-tropmaize", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-tropmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.tropmaize.vcf, data.set = "maize.lmm")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.lmm'): New data set generated 'maize.lmm'
M (data set 'maize.lmm'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}

\cde{st.get.A} obtains \cde{maize.A} directly from the marker data set.

<<tropmaize-prediction-matrix,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.A <- st.get.A("maize.lmm")
@

One plot table retains all genotypes. Responses of the first ten
genotypes are set to \cde{NA}; only observed responses enter the field fit.

<<tropmaize-mask,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.genotypes <- levels(tropmaize$gen)
maize.masked <- tropmaize
maize.masked$y[maize.masked$gen %in% maize.genotypes[1:10]] <- NA
@

\subsubsection{Step 1: Analysis of the field trial}

The field model fits genotype means with a random block effect.

<<tropmaize-two-step-field,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.train.field <- st.lmm(
    fixed = y ~ gen,
    random = ~block,
    data = maize.masked
)
@

The genotype labels and least-squares means form the input to the second analysis.

<<tropmaize-two-step-means,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.means <- as.data.frame(st.lsm(maize.train.field, "gen"))
maize.means <- maize.means[, c("gen", "lsmeans")]
@

\subsubsection{Step 2: Genomic prediction}

The second model uses these means and \cde{maize.A}.

<<tropmaize-two-step-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.two.step <- st.lmm(
    fixed = lsmeans ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    ucov = list(gen = maize.A),
    data = maize.means
)
maize.two.step
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      maize.means
  Fixed:     lsmeans ~ 1
  Random:    ~gen
  Residual:  ~units
  Covariance: gen (264 levels; supplied matrix)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term  DF       LR        p
  gen    1  35.0225  <0.0001

Variance components:
  Component  Variance
  gen          0.3280
  Residual     0.4438

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                254    R-squared (conditional)  0.5613
  Log-likelihood        -346.7073    RMSE (conditional)       0.4931
  AIC                    699.4146    Variance: fixed          0.0000
  BIC                    710.0266    Variance: random         0.5677
  R-squared (marginal)     0.0000    Variance: residual       0.4438
\end{Soutput}

\cde{st.predict} requests predictions for the \cde{gen} term and saves
the result as \cde{maize.two.pred}. Its displayed table includes genotype
labels, predictions, SEs and confidence limits.

<<tropmaize-two-step-predict,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.two.pred <- st.predict(maize.two.step, "gen")
maize.two.pred
@

\begin{Soutput}
Predicted means: fixed intercept + random effect
  gen  prediction      SE   DF  lower.CL  upper.CL
  1        2.4533  0.3425  Inf    1.7820    3.1246
  2        1.9477  0.6873  Inf    0.6007    3.2948
  3        1.2916  0.7218  Inf   -0.1230    2.7062
