Confidence level: 0.9500
SE and intervals use prediction-error variance; fitted variances held
  fixed.
\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Two-step genomic prediction with one training dataset and one prediction dataset}

This example splits the marker data into training and prediction datasets.
The supplied phenotype values are loaded into the training dataset;
the prediction dataset contains markers only.

<<tropmaize-split-load,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
data("v-tropmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.tropmaize.vcf, data.set = "maize.split")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.split'): New data set generated 'maize.split'
M (data set 'maize.split'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}

\cde{st.marker.data.statistics} retrieves the individual identifiers
from the stored marker dataset. The marker and genotype tables are omitted.

<<tropmaize-split-statistics,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.marker.statistics <- st.marker.data.statistics(
    data.set = "maize.split",
    mar = FALSE,
    gen = FALSE
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.split'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}

The first ten stored individuals define the prediction set. The remaining
individuals define the training set.

<<tropmaize-split-ids,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.individuals <- maize.marker.statistics$individual.list$Name
maize.prediction.ids <- maize.individuals[1:10]
maize.training.ids <- maize.individuals[-(1:10)]
@

\cde{st.copy.marker.data} copies the marker dataset. The restriction
retains only the selected training individuals in \cde{"maize.training"}.

<<tropmaize-split-training-markers,eval=FALSE>>=
st.copy.marker.data("maize.training", "maize.split")
st.restrict.marker.data(
    ind.list = maize.training.ids,
    data.set = "maize.training"
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.training'): New data set generated 'maize.training'
M (data set 'maize.training'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
M (data set 'maize.training'): No. of individuals: 254, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}

The second copy retains only the prediction individuals. Both datasets
keep the same marker columns.

<<tropmaize-split-prediction-markers,eval=FALSE>>=
st.copy.marker.data("maize.prediction", "maize.split")
st.restrict.marker.data(
    ind.list = maize.prediction.ids,
    data.set = "maize.prediction"
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.prediction'): New data set generated 'maize.prediction'
M (data set 'maize.prediction'): No. of individuals: 264, no. of markers: 1076
M (data set 'maize.prediction'): No. of individuals: 10, no. of markers: 1076
\end{Soutput}

The field-data analysis results are supplied as genotype phenotype values
in \cde{v-tropmaize-phe}. \cde{st.load.performance.data} loads only
values matching the training marker identifiers.

<<tropmaize-split-performance,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-tropmaize-phe", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.performance.data(
    v.tropmaize.phe,
    data.set = "maize.training"
)
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'maize.training'): No. of individuals: 254, no. of markers: 1076
M (data set 'maize.training'): No. of performance data: 254
\end{Soutput}

With a marker dataset as \cde{data}, the model variables \cde{y} and
\cde{gen} are supplied automatically: \cde{y} denotes the stored
phenotype values and \cde{gen} is the factor identifying individuals.
Both names appear unquoted in the formulas. The quoted \cde{"A"} is
a symbolic covariance specification that constructs the genomic
relationship matrix from the training markers.

<<tropmaize-split-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.training.model <- st.lmm(
    fixed = y ~ 1,
    random = ~gen,
    ucov = list(gen = "A"),
    data = "maize.training"
)
maize.training.model
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      "maize.training"
  Fixed:     y ~ 1
  Random:    ~gen
  Residual:  ~units
  Covariance: gen (254 levels; supplied matrix)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term  DF       LR        p
  gen    1  35.0225  <0.0001

Variance components:
  Component  Variance
  gen          0.3281
  Residual     0.4438

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                254    R-squared (conditional)  0.5612
  Log-likelihood        -346.7073    RMSE (conditional)       0.4931
  AIC                    699.4146    Variance: fixed          0.0000
  BIC                    710.0266    Variance: random         0.5676
  R-squared (marginal)     0.0000    Variance: residual       0.4438
\end{Soutput}

\cde{st.predict} uses the fitted training model and the marker genotypes
in \cde{"maize.prediction"}. Its result contains the prediction
individuals' identifiers and predicted values.

<<tropmaize-split-predict,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.predictions <- st.predict(
    maize.training.model,
    newdata = "maize.prediction"
)
maize.predictions
@

\begin{Soutput}
      gen  prediction
   1  1        2.4533
   2  2        1.9477
   3  3        1.2916
   4  4        1.3752
   5  5        1.7952
   6  6        2.3619
   7  7        2.3133
