The length of the retrieved response vector gives the number of marker
genotypes represented in \cde{maize.y}.

<<tropmaize-dataset-complete,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.marker.genotypes <- length(maize.y)
maize.marker.genotypes
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 264
\end{Soutput}

The missing response entries are counted in
\cde{maize.missing.phenotypes}.

<<tropmaize-dataset-missing-phenotypes,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.missing.phenotypes <- sum(is.na(maize.y))
maize.missing.phenotypes
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 10
\end{Soutput}

The variable \cde{maize.missing.predictions} counts missing entries
in the prediction result.

<<tropmaize-dataset-missing-predictions,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.missing.predictions <- sum(is.na(coef(maize.two.pred)))
maize.missing.predictions
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0
\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Hybrid prediction with SelectionTools marker data sets}

The next example uses stored parental data sets rather than supplying
relationship matrices as R objects. The two parental marker objects and
the hybrid phenotype table are loaded, and the flint markers are stored
under the name \cde{"flint"}.

<<H02-load,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-flintmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-dentmaize-vcf", package = "SelectionTools")
data("v-hybmaize-phe", package = "SelectionTools")
st.load.vcf.data(v.flintmaize.vcf, data.set = "flint")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'flint'): New data set generated 'flint'
M (data set 'flint'): No. of individuals: 86, no. of markers: 35478
\end{Soutput}

The dent marker calls are imported as \cde{"dent"}. These two stored
names match the parental columns in the hybrid performance table.

<<H02-load-b,eval=FALSE>>=
st.load.vcf.data(v.dentmaize.vcf, data.set = "dent")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'dent'): New data set generated 'dent'
M (data set 'dent'): No. of individuals: 123, no. of markers: 35478
\end{Soutput}

The flint marker filter removes markers that are monomorphic within
that pool and retains at most two alleles per marker.

<<H02-load-c,eval=FALSE>>=
st.restrict.marker.data(ExHet.MIN = 1e-8, NoAll.MAX = 2,
                        data.set = "flint")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'flint'): No. of individuals: 86, no. of markers: 28240
\end{Soutput}

The dent marker filter applies the same criteria independently to the
second parental pool.

<<H02-load-d,eval=FALSE>>=
st.restrict.marker.data(ExHet.MIN = 1e-8, NoAll.MAX = 2,
                        data.set = "dent")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'dent'): No. of individuals: 123, no. of markers: 29108
\end{Soutput}

A copy of the hybrid performance table is retained in
\cde{hybrid.performance}. Both responses in its first ten rows are set to
missing, leaving their parental identifiers unchanged.

<<H02-load-e,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.performance <- v.hybmaize.phe
hybrid.performance[seq_len(10), c("GY", "GM")] <- NA_real_
@

The constructor combines that performance frame with references to
\cde{"flint"} and \cde{"dent"} under the name \cde{"maizehybrids"}.
The parental marker calls are shared with those existing data sets.

<<H02-store,eval=FALSE>>=
st.hybrid.data.set(
    markerdata1 = "flint",
    markerdata2 = "dent",
    hybrid.performance = hybrid.performance,
    hybrid.data.set = "maizehybrids"
)
@

The masked records are displayed through \cde{hybrid.missing.rows},
which contains their hybrid identifiers and grain-yield values.

<<H02-missing-rows,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.missing.rows <- hybrid.performance[seq_len(10), c("hybrid", "GY")]
hybrid.missing.rows
@

\begin{Soutput}
    hybrid GY
1  518.298 NA
2  518.305 NA
3  518.306 NA
4  518.316 NA
5  518.323 NA
6  518.327 NA
7  518.437 NA
8  518.439 NA
9  518.444 NA
10 545.298 NA
\end{Soutput}

\subsubsection{GCA only and prediction of all parental combinations}

The fixed formula specifies an intercept for \cde{GY}; the random
formula contains the two parental effects. The two quoted \cde{"A"}
entries obtain their relationships from the linked parental marker data.
\cde{prediction = "Full"} stores predictions for every parental combination.

<<H02-fit,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.gca <- st.lmm(
    fixed = GY ~ 1,
    random = ~flint + dent,
    ucov = list("flint" = "A", "dent" = "A"),
    data = "maizehybrids",
    prediction = "Full"
)
hybrid.gca
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

