  Observations                1244    R-squared (conditional)   0.7997
  Log-likelihood        -3748.7430    RMSE (conditional)        3.1598
  AIC                    7507.4860    Variance: fixed           0.0000
  BIC                    7533.1164    Variance: random         55.4643
  R-squared (marginal)      0.0000    Variance: residual       13.8906
\end{Soutput}


The accessor returns those stored predictions with both parent
identifiers. The \cde{source.row} column identifies the corresponding row
in the retained performance frame.

<<H03-predict,eval=FALSE>>=
sca.predictions <- st.lmm.components(hybrid.gca.sca, "predictions")
sca.predictions
@

\begin{Soutput}
      flint  dent  source.row  prediction
   1  298    518            1     -9.2068
   2  305    518            2     -9.8837
   3  306    518            3    -11.5460
   4  316    518            4      4.2884
   5  323    518            5      0.2847
   6  327    518            6    -12.6736
   7  437    518            7     -5.0252
   8  439    518            8     -7.4678
   9  444    518            9      4.9095
  10  298    545           10     -3.4079
\end{Soutput}

The number of masked yield observations is saved in
\cde{sca.missing.phenotypes}.

<<H03-predict-b,eval=FALSE>>=
sca.missing.phenotypes <- sum(is.na(hybrid.performance$GY))
sca.missing.phenotypes
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 10
\end{Soutput}

The saved prediction column is checked for missing entries, and that
count is displayed through \cde{sca.missing.predictions}.

<<H03-missing-predictions,eval=FALSE>>=
sca.missing.predictions <- sum(is.na(sca.predictions$prediction))
sca.missing.predictions
@

\begin{Soutput}
[1] 0
\end{Soutput}

\subsubsection{Hybrid data inspection}

The listing returns information about the stored hybrid data sets.
\cde{hybrid.status.summary} retains the data-set and parental names,
record and unique-hybrid counts, and the SCA-matrix indicator.

<<H03-list,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.status <- st.hybrid.data.set.list()
hybrid.status.summary <- hybrid.status[
    , c("hybrid.data.set", "markerdata1", "markerdata2",
        "no.records", "no.hybrids", "SCA.matrix")
]
hybrid.status.summary
@

\begin{Soutput}
    hybrid.data.~1 markerdata1 markerdata2 no.records no.hybrids SCA.matrix
  1 maizehybrids   flint       dent           1254.00    1254.00 TRUE      
  hybrid.data.~1 [hybrid.data.set] = hybrid.data.set
\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Genomic prediction with simulated DH pools}
\label{sec:simulated-hybrid-prediction}

The mapped maize data contain 86 flint and 123 dent parents, 8072 shared
markers polymorphic in both pools, and 1254 hybrid performance records
(\litref{technow2014}{Technow et al., 2014};
\litref{melchinger2023}{Melchinger and Frisch, 2023}). This example first
fits the observed hybrid records, then creates simulated DH parents and
crosses, assigns phenotypes to those crosses, and fits the simulated records.
The package data provide the parental marker calls and observed hybrid
responses.

<<H04-load,eval=FALSE>>=
data("v-flintmaize2-vcf")
data("v-dentmaize2-vcf")
data("v-hybmaize2-phe")
@

The three removal calls release hybrid data-set names used in preceding
examples. The parental marker data are then loaded under the names
\cde{"flint"} and \cde{"dent"}.

<<H04-load-remove,eval=FALSE>>=
st.hybrid.data.set.remove("maizehybrids")
st.hybrid.data.set.remove("maizeTRUE")
st.hybrid.data.set.remove("maizeTS")
@

Each \cde{data.set} argument names the marker data set created by the
load call. The two data sets retain their separate parental identifiers.

<<H04-load-markers,eval=FALSE>>=
st.load.vcf.data(v.flintmaize2.vcf, data.set = "flint")
st.load.vcf.data(v.dentmaize2.vcf, data.set = "dent")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'flint'): No. of individuals: 86, no. of markers: 8072
M (data set 'dent'): No. of individuals: 123, no. of markers: 8072
\end{Soutput}

\cde{st.hybrid.data.set} associates the observed hybrid records with the
two parental marker data sets. Its last argument gives this hybrid data
set the name \cde{"maizeTRUE"}.

<<H04-hybrid-data,eval=FALSE>>=
st.hybrid.data.set("flint", "dent", v.hybmaize2.phe, "maizeTRUE")
@

The model uses \cde{GY} as its response. The terms \cde{flint} and
\cde{dent} identify parental effects, and \cde{flint:dent} identifies the
cross-specific effect. The \cde{ucov} entries request parental relationship
matrices with \cde{"A"} and their cross relationship with
\cde{"kro(a,a)"}. The fitted object is stored in \cde{maize.founder.model};
the hybrid data set also retains the information needed for later prediction.

<<H04-fit-real,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.founder.model <- st.lmm(
    GY ~ 1,
    random = ~flint + dent + flint:dent,
    ucov = list(flint = "A", dent = "A", "flint:dent" = "kro(a,a)"),
    data = "maizeTRUE"
)
maize.founder.model
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      "maizeTRUE"
  Fixed:     GY ~ 1
  Random:    ~flint + dent + flint:dent
  Residual:  ~units
  Covariance: flint (86 levels; supplied matrix)
  Covariance: dent (123 levels; supplied matrix)
  Covariance: flint:dent (1254 levels; supplied matrix)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term        DF        LR        p
  flint        1  551.5060  <0.0001
  dent         1  700.7613  <0.0001
  flint:dent   1   45.8906  <0.0001

Variance components:
  Component   Variance
  flint        10.8961
  dent         15.3697
  flint:dent    1.3144
  Residual     13.4673

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                1254    R-squared (conditional)   0.8029
  Log-likelihood        -3772.9671    RMSE (conditional)        3.0813
  AIC                    7555.9342    Variance: fixed           0.0000
  BIC                    7581.6047    Variance: random         54.8625
  R-squared (marginal)      0.0000    Variance: residual       13.4673
\end{Soutput}

The four variables specify the total marker count after map extension,
the number of DH lines in each pool, the number of simulated hybrid
records, and the target \cde{h2} used to generate phenotypes.

<<H04-simulate,eval=FALSE>>=
n.markers <- 9000L
n.dh      <- 100L
n.hybrids <- 200L
h2        <- 0.8
@

The thread setting and seed reproduce the simulation configuration.
\cde{reset.all()} clears existing simulation populations and genome settings
before the marker data are transferred.

<<H04-simulate-initialize,eval=FALSE>>=
st.set.num.threads(1)
rng.init(seed = 20261024L)
reset.all()
@

\cde{st.set.sim.gp} defines the simulation genome from the flint map,
and \cde{st.set.sim.mp} copies its mapped loci. The two
\cde{st.set.sim.pp} calls create founder populations from the parental
marker data. \cde{extend.marker.map} increases the total to 9000 loci
while retaining the original marker identifiers and positions.

<<H04-simulate-map,eval=FALSE>>=
st.set.sim.gp("flint")
st.set.sim.mp("flint")
st.set.sim.pp("simFlint", "flint")
st.set.sim.pp("simDent", "dent")
extend.marker.map(n.markers)
@

\begin{Soutput}
M: 10 chromosomes defined
M: 8072 loci defined
\end{Soutput}

Each \cde{cross} call creates an F1 population by random mating within
one founder pool. The setting \cde{mode = 0} excludes selection of the
same individual as both parents. Each \cde{dh} call samples parents
from that F1 population and produces 100 DH lines. The names distinguish
the F1 and DH populations in the simulation.

<<H04-simulate-dh,eval=FALSE>>=
cross("simFlintF1", "simFlint", "simFlint", n.dh, mode = 0)
cross("simDentF1", "simDent", "simDent", n.dh, mode = 0)
dh("simflintDH", "simFlintF1", n.dh)
dh("simdentDH", "simDentF1", n.dh)
@

\cde{st.get.simpop} exports the simulated marker calls, identifiers and
pedigrees to marker data sets. The resulting names \cde{"TSflint"} and
\cde{"TSdent"} are used by the subsequent hybrid analysis. The added
marker calls follow the inherited chromosome segments.

<<H04-simulate-export,eval=FALSE>>=
st.get.simpop("simflintDH", data.set = "TSflint")
st.get.simpop("simdentDH", data.set = "TSdent")
@

\begin{Soutput}
M (data set 'TSflint'): New data set generated 'TSflint'
M (data set 'TSflint'): No. of individuals: 100, no. of markers: 9000
M (data set 'TSdent'): New data set generated 'TSdent'
M (data set 'TSdent'): No. of individuals: 100, no. of markers: 9000
\end{Soutput}

The pedigree accessor reads the exported flint marker data set.
\cde{flint.pedigree} contains its first three identifier and pedigree records.

<<H04-pedigree,eval=FALSE>>=
flint.pedigree <- head(
    population.pedigree("TSflint", source = "marker"),
    3
)
flint.pedigree
@

\begin{Soutput}
                           name        pedigree
1 simflintDH.410((338_x_430)DH) ((338_x_430)DH)
2 simflintDH.411((332_x_354)DH) ((332_x_354)DH)
3 simflintDH.412((347_x_316)DH) ((347_x_316)DH)
\end{Soutput}

\cde{population.cross} creates 200 random crosses and returns their
hybrid and parental identifiers. The renamed columns match the two
exported marker data sets. \cde{st.hybrid.data.set} then makes this
crossing design available as \cde{"maizeTS"}.

<<H04-crosses,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.design <- population.cross(
    "simHybrids", "simflintDH", "simdentDH", n.hybrids
)
names(hybrid.design) <- c("hybrid", "TSflint", "TSdent")
st.hybrid.data.set("TSflint", "TSdent", hybrid.design, "maizeTS")
@

Prediction uses the model retained with \cde{"maizeTRUE"} for the
crosses named in \cde{"maizeTS"}. It matches the 8072 markers used in
the original fit. The prediction table supplies the values and parental
identifiers to \cde{st.generate.phenotypes}, which adds random residuals
and stores the generated response as \cde{GY} in \cde{"maizeTS"}.
With \cde{h2 = 0.8}, the added residual variance is one quarter of the
sample variance of the supplied predictions.

<<H04-crosses-phenotypes,eval=FALSE>>=
hybrid.predictions <- as.data.frame(
    st.predict("maizeTRUE", newdata = "maizeTS")
)
st.generate.phenotypes("maizeTS", hybrid.predictions, h2 = h2, trait = "GY")
@

The second fit uses all 200 generated responses. Its parental and
cross-specific random terms refer to the exported DH marker data sets.
Their symbolic relationship specifications now use the complete
9000-marker panel. \cde{maize.simulated.model} contains this fitted model.

<<H04-fit-sim,eval=FALSE>>=
maize.simulated.model <- st.lmm(
    GY ~ 1,
    random = ~TSflint + TSdent + TSflint:TSdent,
    ucov = list(
        TSflint = "A", TSdent = "A",
        "TSflint:TSdent" = "kro(a,a)"
    ),
    data = "maizeTS"
)
maize.simulated.model
@

\begin{Soutput}
Linear mixed model fitted by REML

Model:
  Data:      "maizeTS"
  Fixed:     GY ~ 1
  Random:    ~TSflint + TSdent + TSflint:TSdent
  Residual:  ~units
  Covariance: TSflint (100 levels; supplied matrix)
  Covariance: TSdent (100 levels; supplied matrix)
  Covariance: TSflint:TSdent (196 levels; supplied matrix)

Convergence:
  converged: projected score tolerance met

Random effects (likelihood-ratio tests):
  Term            DF        LR        p
  TSflint          1   77.4058  <0.0001
  TSdent           1  110.7390  <0.0001
  TSflint:TSdent   1    0.6904   0.4060

Variance components:
  Component       Variance
  TSflint           4.5291
  TSdent            6.5047
  TSflint:TSdent    0.5622
  Residual          4.0426

Fit and model statistics:
  Observations                200    R-squared (conditional)   0.8575
  Log-likelihood        -567.2473    RMSE (conditional)        1.1600
  AIC                   1144.4947    Variance: fixed           0.0000
  BIC                   1160.9863    Variance: random         24.3238
  R-squared (marginal)     0.0000    Variance: residual        4.0426
\end{Soutput}

The final call predicts the records in \cde{"maizeTS"} from its retained
model. \cde{coef} extracts their genetic values, and
\cde{first.genotypic.values} contains the first six rows for display.

<<H04-predict,eval=FALSE>>=
genotypic.values <- coef(st.predict("maizeTS"))
first.genotypic.values <- head(genotypic.values)
first.genotypic.values
@

\begin{Soutput}
  Row     Value
  1     -2.5119
  2     -9.3634
  3    -13.1846
  4     -0.0778
  5     -4.4069
  6      2.8736
\end{Soutput}

\section{References}
\label{sec:references}

\begingroup
\def\lit{\noindent\hangindent\parindent}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{3em}
\clubpenalty=10000
\widowpenalty=10000

\subsection{Experimental designs and analysis of variance}

Completely randomized designs, randomized blocks, Latin squares, factorial
experiments, split plots, incomplete blocks, lattices and expected mean
squares.

\bigskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:cochran1941} Cochran WG (1941) The distribution of the largest of a set of estimated
  variances as a fraction of their total. Annals of Eugenics 11:47--52
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Cochran's C test for homogeneity of cell variances}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:cornfield1956} Cornfield J, Tukey JW (1956) Average values of mean squares in factorials.
  Annals of Mathematical Statistics 27:907--949
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{The original reference for the Cornfield--Tukey rules.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:fisher1925} Fisher RA (1925) Statistical methods for research workers. 1st edn. Oliver
  and Boyd, Edinburgh
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Chapter VIII, \S49, explicitly develops the Latin square as an
  experimental design and gives its ANOVA.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:fisher1926} Fisher RA (1926) The arrangement of field experiments. Journal of the
  Ministry of Agriculture of Great Britain 33:503--513
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{A foundational original paper on randomization, blocking and factorial
  experimentation.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:fisher1935} Fisher RA (1935) The design of experiments. 1st edn. Oliver and Boyd,
  Edinburgh
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{The foundational systematic treatment of experimental design, including
  randomized blocks, Latin squares and factorial experiments.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:fletcher2002} Fletcher DJ, Underwood AJ (2002) How to cope with negative estimates of
  components of variance in ecological field studies. Journal of Experimental
  Marine Biology and Ecology 273:89--95
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Explicit pooling and the interpretation of negative component
  estimates}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:patterson1976} Patterson HD, Williams ER (1976) A new class of resolvable incomplete block
  designs. Biometrika 63:83--92
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Alpha-lattice designs}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:underwood1997} Underwood AJ (1997) Experiments in ecology: their logical design and
  interpretation using analysis of variance. Cambridge University Press,
  Cambridge
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Particularly relevant to the balanced crossed, nested and split-plot
  examples.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:yates1935} Yates F (1935) Complex experiments. Supplement to the Journal of the Royal
  Statistical Society 2:181--223
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{An early detailed treatment, including the oats experiment used in the
  vignette.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:yates1936a} Yates F (1936) A new method of arranging variety trials involving a large
  number of varieties. Journal of Agricultural Science 26:424--455
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Introduces the arrangements underlying standard lattice designs.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:yates1936b} Yates F (1936) Incomplete randomized blocks. Annals of Eugenics 7:121--140
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{An original reference for incomplete-block construction and analysis.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:yates1937} Yates F (1937) The design and analysis of factorial experiments. Technical
  Communication No.~35, Imperial Bureau of Soil Science, Harpenden
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Covers factorial arrangements, confounding and split plots.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:yates1940} Yates F (1940) Lattice squares. Journal of Agricultural Science 30:672--687
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Develops their analysis, including recovery of inter-row and
  inter-column information.}\par\medskip

\subsection{Linear mixed models and estimation}

Variance components, mixed-model equations, BLUE and BLUP, ML and REML,
optimization algorithms and random regression.

\bigskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:lapack1999} Anderson E, Bai Z, Bischof C, Blackford S, Demmel J, Dongarra J, Du Croz
  J, Greenbaum A, Hammarling S, McKenney A, Sorensen D (1999) LAPACK users'
  guide. 3rd edn. SIAM, Philadelphia
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Dense linear algebra, least squares, factorizations,
  eigensystems and BLAS}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:byrd1995} Byrd RH, Lu P, Nocedal J, Zhu C (1995) A limited memory algorithm for bound
  constrained optimization. SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 16:1190--1208
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{L-BFGS-B optimization}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:davis2006} Davis TA (2006) Direct methods for sparse linear systems. SIAM,
  Philadelphia
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Sparse direct factorization and triangular solves}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:deboor2001} de Boor C (2001) A practical guide to splines. Revised edn. Springer, New
  York
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Spline bases used in regression and prediction}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:draper1998} Draper NR, Smith H (1998) Applied regression analysis. 3rd edn. Wiley,
  New York
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Least squares, polynomial regression, pure error and lack-of-fit
  tests}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:gilmour1995} Gilmour AR, Thompson R, Cullis BR (1995) Average information REML: an
  efficient algorithm for variance parameter estimation in linear mixed
  models. Biometrics 51:1440--1450
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Average-information REML}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:harville1977} Harville DA (1977) Maximum likelihood approaches to variance component
  estimation and to related problems. Journal of the American Statistical
  Association 72:320--338
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Likelihood estimation with structured covariance components}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:henderson1975} Henderson CR (1975) Best linear unbiased estimation and prediction under a
  selection model. Biometrics 31:423--447
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{BLUE, BLUP and mixed-model equations}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:laird1982} Laird NM, Ware JH (1982) Random-effects models for longitudinal data.
  Biometrics 38:963--974
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Relevant to the chicken-growth analyses.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:mclean1991} McLean RA, Sanders WL, Stroup WW
  (1991) A unified approach to mixed linear models. The American
  Statistician 45:54--64. \href{https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1991.10475767}{doi:10.1080/00031305.1991.10475767}
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Linear functions of fixed and random effects and their inference spaces.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:patterson1971} Patterson HD, Thompson R (1971) Recovery of inter-block information when
  block sizes are unequal. Biometrika 58:545--554
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{REML estimation}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:sanders1991} Sanders WL (1991) The use of the
  SAS system in statistical training. Proceedings of the Sixteenth Annual
  SAS Users Group International Conference
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Machine and operator data and the inference-space comparisons.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:searle1987} Searle SR (1987) Linear models for unbalanced data. Wiley, New York
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Coding, estimability, adjusted sums of squares and general
  linear hypotheses}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:searle1992} Searle SR, Casella G, McCulloch CE (1992) Variance components. Wiley, New
  York
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Covers moment estimation, ML, REML and prediction in mixed models.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:wilkinson1973} Wilkinson GN, Rogers CE (1973) Symbolic description of factorial models
  for analysis of variance. Applied Statistics 22:392--399
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{The original symbolic model-formula notation}\par\medskip

\subsection{Statistical inference and model assessment}

Satterthwaite and Kenward--Roger methods, bootstrap tests, Hotelling's
\(T^2\), MANOVA, AIC, BIC and mixed-model \(R^2\).

\bigskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:akaike1974} Akaike H (1974) A new look at the statistical model identification. IEEE
  Transactions on Automatic Control 19:716--723
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Akaike information criterion}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:anderson2003} Anderson TW (2003) An introduction to multivariate statistical analysis. 3rd
  edn. Wiley, Hoboken
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Covers the multivariate linear model and the Wilks, Pillai,
  Hotelling--Lawley and Roy criteria.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:davison1997} Davison AC, Hinkley DV (1997) Bootstrap methods and their application.
  Cambridge University Press, Cambridge
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Relevant to simulation under the reduced model and refitting both
  models.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:fai1996} Fai AHT, Cornelius PL (1996) Approximate F-tests of multiple degree of
  freedom hypotheses in generalized least squares analyses of unbalanced
  split-plot experiments. Journal of Statistical Computation and Simulation
  54:363--378
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Multiple-degree-of-freedom approximate F-tests}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:hotelling1931} Hotelling H (1931) The generalization of Student's ratio. Annals of
  Mathematical Statistics 2:360--378
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Hotelling's \(T^2\) and its multivariate F reference}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:johnson2014} Johnson PCD (2014) Extension of Nakagawa \& Schielzeth's
  \(R^2_{\mathrm{GLMM}}\) to random slopes models. Methods in Ecology and
  Evolution 5:944--946
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Relevant to observation-averaged random-effect variances.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:kenward1997} Kenward MG, Roger JH (1997) Small sample inference for fixed effects from
  restricted maximum likelihood. Biometrics 53:983--997
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Supports covariance adjustment, F scaling and denominator degrees of
  freedom.}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:nakagawa2013} Nakagawa S, Schielzeth H (2013) A general and simple method for obtaining
  \(R^2\) from generalized linear mixed-effects models. Methods in Ecology and
  Evolution 4:133--142
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Marginal and conditional \(R^2\)}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:satterthwaite1946} Satterthwaite FE (1946) An approximate distribution of estimates of variance
  components. Biometrics Bulletin 2:110--114
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Satterthwaite degrees of freedom}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:schwarz1978} Schwarz G (1978) Estimating the dimension of a model. Annals of Statistics
  6:461--464
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Bayesian information criterion}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:self1987} Self SG, Liang KY (1987) Asymptotic properties of maximum likelihood
  estimators and likelihood ratio tests under nonstandard conditions. Journal
  of the American Statistical Association 82:605--610
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Explains why variance-boundary likelihood-ratio inference is
  nonstandard}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:wald1943} Wald A (1943) Tests of statistical hypotheses concerning several
  parameters when the number of observations is large. Transactions of the
  American Mathematical Society 54:426--482
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Wald tests and large-sample normal inference}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:wilks1938} Wilks SS (1938) The large-sample distribution of the likelihood ratio for
  testing composite hypotheses. Annals of Mathematical Statistics 9:60--62
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{The regular asymptotic chi-squared likelihood-ratio reference}\par\medskip

\subsection{Least-squares means, contrasts and multiple comparisons}

Least-squares means, reference grids, transformations, simultaneous
inference, multiplicity adjustments and compact letter displays.

\bigskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:bh1995} Benjamini Y, Hochberg Y (1995) Controlling the false discovery rate: a
  practical and powerful approach to multiple testing. Journal of the Royal
  Statistical Society, Series B 57:289--300
  \\*[\smallskipamount]
  \textit{Benjamini--Hochberg false discovery rate}\par\medskip

\lit \phantomsection\label{lit:by2001} Benjamini Y, Yekutieli D (2001) The control of the false discovery rate in
  multiple testing under dependency. Annals of Statistics 29:1165--1188
